|~ `::any`, the kind beneath every kind, and what is declared under it:
   the verbs every value answers.
   The rules it holds: [QSDSP#residence], [QSPRN#print], [QSPRN#excerpt]. ~|

:any
  |~~ Any is the kind beneath every kind, where the verbs every value
      answers live: `tag`, `eq`, `doc`, `print`, `json`, `excerpt`,
      `if` and their kin. A verb that means the same for every
      value is written once here, and the walk from a name to its verb
      ends here, after the kinds of the subject.

      No value is of this kind alone, since `tag` answers the kind
      above it, and every value answers the verbs listed here.
      ::snippet~(::any | manifest) lists the verbs of every value.
      ::link~(::qlang/verbs | doc)
  ~~|
  ::kind
    | throws :UnknownNameError
        |~~ A name an axis of a name was asked for that names nothing the
            scope declares is refused under the axis,
            `::any/doc/UnknownNameError` for `doc`, and so for `binding`,
            `source`, `manifest`, `laws` and `runLaws`: every such refusal
            is closed by the kind of this one.
            ::link~(::qlang/hypertext | doc)
        ~~|
        ~(wires :name |~~ The name asked for, a keyword or a tag. ~~| #[::keyword ::tag]
          | wires :addresses
              |~~ The addresses where a verb of that name lives: one for each kind that answers it. ~~|
              ::set
          | wires :nearest
              |~~ The names the step could have meant: nearest in spelling, `[]` where none is near. ~~|
              ::vec)
    | proves :holdsTag
        |~~ `tag` lives on `::any`. ~~|
        ~(::any | manifest | has ::any/tag)
    | proves :isTheKindOfNoValue
        |~~ No value's tag is `::any`. ~~|
        ~(5 | tag | eq ::any | not)
    | proves :isTheKindOfEveryValue
        |~~ Every value is of `::any`, so laying it changes nothing. ~~|
        ~([5 ::Box#5 #[1]] | map ~(:v it | get [~(is ::any) ~(tag ::any | eq v)]) | eq [[true true] [true true] [true true]])

:any/desc
  |~~ Turns the subject: a value under `::desc` back to its payload, and
      any other under `::desc`, as `tag ::desc` lays it, `5 | desc`
      answering `::desc#5` and `5 | desc | desc` answering `5`. A value
      turned ranks the greater first: a key of `sort`, `min` or `max`
      that answers it descends,
      `[1 3 2] | sort ~(desc)` answering `[3 2 1]`, and in a vector key it
      turns its own index alone, `sort ~(get [~(get /n | desc) /s])`. A
      value of every kind turns, a map among them, and `payload` reads it
      back. The key is code, a
      quote: `sort desc` turns the vector itself, which `sort` refuses
      as no key. A key turned in `max` finds the least. The call takes no
      value; a field turns by reading it first, `~(get /n | desc)`.
      ::link~(::desc | doc) ::link~(::any/tag | doc)
      ::link~(::vec/sort | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns
        |~~ The subject turned: under `::desc`, which ranks the greater
            first, or a value turned back to its payload.
        ~~|
        ::any
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `desc` turns its subject and takes no value: a key turns a
            field by reading it first, `sort ~(get /n | desc)`.
        ~~|
    | proves :answersADesc
        |~~ `desc` answers the subject under `::desc`. ~~|
        ~(5 | desc | tag | eq ::desc)
    | proves :descendsASort
        |~~ A sort by `desc` descends. ~~|
        ~([1 3 2] | sort ~(desc) | eq [3 2 1])
    | proves :turnsOneIndexOfAVectorKey
        |~~ In a vector key `desc` turns its own index alone. ~~|
        ~([{:n 1 :s "b"} {:n 1 :s "a"} {:n 2 :s "c"}]
          | sort ~(get [~(get /n | desc) /s]) | map /s | eq ["c" "a" "b"])
    | proves :readsBackByPayload
        |~~ A map turns, and `payload` reads it back. ~~|
        ~({:n 1} | desc | payload | eq {:n 1})
    | proves :turnsTheWholeVectorWithoutAQuote
        |~~ `sort desc` turns the vector, which `sort` refuses as no key.
        ~~|
        ~([1 3 2] | sort desc
          !| get [~(tag) ~(actualTag)]
          |  eq [::vec/sort/key/KindNotTakenError ::desc])
    | proves :turnsBackToTheValue
        |~~ A value turned twice is the value. ~~|
        ~([5 {:n 1} ::Box[2]] | map ~(:v it | desc | desc | eq v) | every ~(it))
    | proves :turnsAMaximum
        |~~ A key turned in `max` finds the least. ~~|
        ~([{:n 1} {:n 2}] | max ~(get /n | desc) | eq {:n 1})
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesAField
        |~~ A field given to `desc` is refused under the verb. ~~|
        ~([{:n 1} {:n 2}] | sort ~(desc (get /n)) !| tag | eq ::any/desc/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | impl ~(if (tag | eq ::desc) ~(payload) ~(tag ::desc))

:any/eq
  |~~ Answers whether the subject and the value the call gives are one
      value: of one kind, under the same tags, and equal part for part,
      the order of a map's keys and of a set's elements aside. So a
      keyword is not its string, `:a` and `"a"`; a vector is not the set
      of its elements, `[1 2]` and `#[1 2]`; a value under a tag is not
      its payload; and a number equals itself whatever its digits, `1` and
      `1.0`, while a sum of doubles may miss, `0.1 | add 0.2`. The value
      is computed against the subject, so two fields of one record are
      compared through a name, `:r it | get /a | eq (r | get /b)`, and in
      a condition the subject is the element,
      `filter ~(get /status | eq "open")`. `ne` answers the opposite.
      ::link~(::any/ne | doc) ::link~(::qlang/values | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :value
        |~~ The value compared with the subject, any value, computed
            against the subject.
        ~~|
        /0
    | throws :value/NotGivenError
        |~~ `eq` compares the subject with the value its call gives,
            `5 | eq 5`; a call with no value is refused.
        ~~|
    | returns
        |~~ `true` when the subject and the value are one value, `false`
            otherwise.
        ~~|
        ::boolean
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `eq` compares the subject with one value and was given more:
            whether the subject is one of several is
            `:x it | [5 6] | contains x`.
        ~~|
    | proves :comparesPartForPart
        |~~ Nested values compare part for part, a set without its order.
        ~~|
        ~({:a [1 #[2 3]]} | eq {:a [1 #[3 2]]})
    | proves :keepsAKeywordApartFromItsString
        |~~ A keyword is not its string. ~~|
        ~(:a | eq "a" | not)
    | proves :keepsAVectorApartFromItsSet
        |~~ A vector is not the set of its elements. ~~|
        ~([1 2] | eq #[1 2] | not)
    | proves :keepsATagOverItsPayload
        |~~ A value under a tag is not its payload. ~~|
        ~(::Box[1] | eq [1] | not)
    | proves :readsANumberByItsValue
        |~~ A number equals itself whatever its digits. ~~|
        ~(1 | eq 1.0)
    | proves :comparesDoublesAsTheyAdd
        |~~ A sum of doubles may miss the number it is written as. ~~|
        ~(0.1 | add 0.2 | eq 0.3 | not)
    | proves :comparesTwoFieldsThroughAName
        |~~ Two fields of one record compare through a name. ~~|
        ~({:a 1 :b 1} | :r it | get /a | eq (r | get /b))
    | proves :value/NotGivenError/refusesACallWithNoValue
        |~~ A call with no value is refused under the input. ~~|
        ~(5 | eq !| tag | eq ::any/eq/value/NotGivenError)
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesTwoValues
        |~~ A call with two values is refused under the verb. ~~|
        ~(5 | eq 5 6 !| tag | eq ::any/eq/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | proves :keepsTheOrderOfKeysAside
        |~~ Two maps of the same entries are one value, whatever the order
            of their keys.
        ~~|
        ~({:a 1 :b 2} | eq {:b 2 :a 1})
    | impl ::builtin{}

:any/ne
  |~~ Answers whether the subject and the value the call gives differ, by
      the equality of `eq`, whose opposite it is: a keyword differs from
      its string, a vector from the set of its elements, a value under a
      tag from its payload.
      ::link~(::any/eq | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :value
        |~~ The value compared with the subject, any value, computed
            against the subject.
        ~~|
        /0
    | throws :value/NotGivenError
        |~~ `ne` compares the subject with the value its call gives,
            `5 | ne 6`; a call with no value is refused.
        ~~|
    | returns
        |~~ `true` when the subject and the value differ, `false` when
            they are one value.
        ~~|
        ::boolean
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `ne` compares the subject with one value, `5 | ne 6`; a call
            with more is refused.
        ~~|
    | proves :answersTheOppositeOfEq
        |~~ Two values differ exactly when they are not one value. ~~|
        ~([[1 2] [1 1] [:a "a"]] | map ~(:p it | get /0 | ne (p | get /1)) | eq [true false true])
    | proves :value/NotGivenError/refusesACallWithNoValue
        |~~ A call with no value is refused under the input. ~~|
        ~(5 | ne !| tag | eq ::any/ne/value/NotGivenError)
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesTwoValues
        |~~ A call with two values is refused under the verb. ~~|
        ~(5 | ne 6 7 !| tag | eq ::any/ne/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:any/gt
  |~~ Answers whether the subject comes after the value the call gives in
      the one order, the order `sort` ranks by, the two of one kind, as
      `tag` answers it: numbers by their value, `10 | gt 5`, a decimal
      against a whole number among them; strings by their characters,
      `"b" | gt "a"`, an upper-case letter before every lower-case one and
      digits as characters, `"10" | gt "9"` answering `false`; keywords
      and tag names by their names; values under `::desc` the other way,
      `1 | desc | gt (2 | desc)`; and vectors, sets, maps, booleans and
      values under one tag as `sort` ranks them. So `gt` answers `true`
      exactly where `sort` puts the value before the subject. A pair of two
      kinds is refused: a number under a tag is read by `payload`,
      `::Width#5 | payload | gt 3`, and text that holds a number by
      `toNumber`, `get /age | toNumber | gt 18`. The value is computed
      against the subject, and in a condition the subject is the element,
      `filter ~(gt 1)`. `gte` answers whether the subject comes after the
      value or ranks with it, `lt` and `lte` the reverse.
      ::link~(::any/gte | doc) ::link~(::any/lt | doc)
      ::link~(::any/lte | doc) ::link~(::vec/sort | doc)
      ::link~(::desc | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :value
        |~~ The value the subject is compared with, of the subject's kind,
            computed against the subject, `5 | gt 3`.
        ~~|
        /0
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `gt` compares the subject with one value: two fields of one
            record compare through a name, `:r it | get /a | gt (r | get /b)`.
        ~~|
    | returns
        |~~ `true` when the subject comes after the value, `false`
            otherwise.
        ~~|
        ::boolean
    | proves :comparesNumbers |~~ Ten is greater than five. ~~| ~(10 | gt 5)
    | proves :comparesADecimal
        |~~ A whole number compares with a decimal. ~~|
        ~(3 | gt 2.5)
    | proves :keepsOneValueApartFromItself
        |~~ A value does not come after itself. ~~|
        ~(5 | gt 5 | not)
    | proves :ordersStringsByCharacters
        |~~ Strings compare by their characters, an upper-case letter
            before every lower-case one and digits as characters.
        ~~|
        ~(get [~("b" | gt "a") ~("B" | gt "a") ~("10" | gt "9")] | eq [true false false])
    | proves :comparesKeywordsAndTagNamesByName
        |~~ Keywords and tag names compare by their names. ~~|
        ~(get [~(:b | gt :a) ~(::B | gt ::A)] | eq [true true])
    | proves :comparesTurnedValuesTheOtherWay
        |~~ Values under `::desc` compare the other way. ~~|
        ~(1 | desc | gt (2 | desc))
    | proves :comparesAsSortRanks
        |~~ Vectors, booleans and values under one tag compare as `sort`
            ranks them.
        ~~|
        ~(get [~([1 2] | gt [1]) ~(true | gt false) ~(::Width#5 | gt ::Width#3)] | eq [true true true])
    | proves :answersWhereSortPutsTheValueFirst
        |~~ The subject comes after the value where `sort` puts the value
            first, and not where it puts the subject first.
        ~~|
        ~(get [~(::Width#5 | gt ::Width#3) ~([::Width#5 ::Width#3] | sort | first) ~("B" | gt "a") ~(["B" "a"] | sort | first)]
          | eq [true ::Width#3 false "B"])
    | proves :filtersByTheElement
        |~~ In a condition the subject is the element. ~~|
        ~([1 2 3] | filter ~(gt 1) | eq [2 3])
    | proves :refusesANumberUnderATag
        |~~ A number under a tag beside a number is refused with the two
            kinds, and its payload compares.
        ~~|
        ~(get [~(::Width#5 | gt 3 !| get [~(tag) ~(leftTag) ~(rightTag)]) ~(::Width#5 | payload | gt 3)]
          | eq [[::call/KindsMismatchedError ::Width ::number] true])
    | proves :refusesTextBesideANumber
        |~~ Text that holds a number is read by `toNumber` first. ~~|
        ~(get [~("5" | gt 3 !| tag) ~("5" | toNumber | gt 3)] | eq [::call/KindsMismatchedError true])
    | proves :refusesTwoTags
        |~~ Two values under two tags are refused. ~~|
        ~(::Cents#100 | gt ::Dollars#50 !| get [~(leftTag) ~(rightTag)] | eq [::Cents ::Dollars])
    | impl ::builtin{}

:any/gte
  |~~ Answers whether the subject comes after the value the call gives or
      ranks with it in the one order, `10 | gte 10`, the two of one kind as
      `gt` compares them, so a whole number ranks with its decimal,
      `5 | gte 5.0`.
      ::link~(::any/gt | doc) ::link~(::any/lte | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :value
        |~~ The value the subject is compared with, of the subject's kind,
            computed against the subject, `5 | gte 3`.
        ~~|
        /0
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `gte` compares the subject with one value, as `gt` does: two
            fields of one record compare through a name.
        ~~|
    | returns
        |~~ `true` when the subject comes after the value or ranks with it,
            `false` otherwise.
        ~~|
        ::boolean
    | proves :holdsOfItself |~~ A value ranks with itself. ~~| ~(10 | gte 10)
    | proves :holdsOfItsDecimal
        |~~ A whole number ranks with its decimal. ~~|
        ~(5 | gte 5.0)
    | proves :failsBelow
        |~~ A smaller number is not at least the value. ~~|
        ~(2 | gte 3 | not)
    | proves :refusesAString
        |~~ A string is no number to compare with. ~~|
        ~(5 | gte "3" !| tag | eq ::call/KindsMismatchedError)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:any/lt
  |~~ Answers whether the subject comes before the value the call gives in
      the one order, `5 | lt 10`, the two of one kind as `gt` compares
      them, the empty string before every other; `lte` answers whether it
      comes before the value or ranks with it.
      ::link~(::any/gt | doc) ::link~(::any/lte | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :value
        |~~ The value the subject is compared with, of the subject's kind,
            computed against the subject, `5 | lt 3`.
        ~~|
        /0
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `lt` compares the subject with one value, as `gt` does: two
            fields of one record compare through a name.
        ~~|
    | returns
        |~~ `true` when the subject comes before the value, `false`
            otherwise.
        ~~|
        ::boolean
    | proves :comparesNumbers |~~ Five is less than ten. ~~| ~(5 | lt 10)
    | proves :keepsOneValueApartFromItself
        |~~ A value does not come before itself. ~~|
        ~(5 | lt 5 | not)
    | proves :putsTheEmptyStringFirst
        |~~ The empty string comes before every other. ~~|
        ~("" | lt "a")
    | proves :refusesAString
        |~~ A string is no number to compare with. ~~|
        ~(5 | lt "3" !| tag | eq ::call/KindsMismatchedError)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:any/lte
  |~~ Answers whether the subject comes before the value the call gives or
      ranks with it in the one order, `10 | lte 10`, the two of one kind as
      `gt` compares them.
      ::link~(::any/lt | doc) ::link~(::any/gte | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :value
        |~~ The value the subject is compared with, of the subject's kind,
            computed against the subject, `5 | lte 3`.
        ~~|
        /0
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `lte` compares the subject with one value, as `gt` does: two
            fields of one record compare through a name.
        ~~|
    | returns
        |~~ `true` when the subject comes before the value or ranks with
            it, `false` otherwise.
        ~~|
        ::boolean
    | proves :holdsOfItself |~~ A value ranks with itself. ~~| ~(10 | lte 10)
    | proves :failsAbove
        |~~ A greater number is not at most the value. ~~|
        ~(2 | lte 1 | not)
    | proves :refusesAString
        |~~ A string is no number to compare with. ~~|
        ~(5 | lte "3" !| tag | eq ::call/KindsMismatchedError)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:any/type
  |~~ The name other languages give the read of `tag`: answers the kind of
      the subject, its outermost tag, as `tag` given no name does,
      `5 | type` answering `::number` and `::Width#5 | type` `::Width`.
      The answer is a tag name, a value that reads the kind's page,
      `type | doc`, and compares, `filter ~(type | eq ::string)`. The call
      takes no value: whether the subject is of a kind is `is`.
      ::link~(::any/tag | doc) ::link~(::any/is | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ The kind of the subject, its outermost tag. ~~| ::tag
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `type` answers the kind of its subject and takes no value:
            whether the subject is of a kind is `is`, `5 | is ::number`.
        ~~|
    | proves :answersAsTag
        |~~ `type` answers as `tag` given no name. ~~|
        ~([5 ::Width#5 #[1]] | map ~(get [~(type) ~(tag)]) | eq [[::number ::number] [::Width ::Width] [::set ::set]])
    | proves :readsThePageOfItsKind
        |~~ The answer reads the page of the kind. ~~|
        ~(5 | type | doc | eq (::number | doc))
    | proves :comparesAsATagName
        |~~ The answer compares with a tag name. ~~|
        ~(["a" 1 "b"] | filter ~(type | eq ::string) | eq ["a" "b"])
    | proves :readsAnErrorAtRest
        |~~ An error passes over `type`, and the error at rest answers
            the tag of its refusal.
        ~~|
        ~("x" | add 1 | type !| type | eq ::number/add/SubjectNotNumberError)
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesAKind
        |~~ A kind given to `type` is refused under the verb. ~~|
        ~(5 | type ::number !| type | eq ::any/type/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | impl ~(tag)

:any/json
  |~~ Answers the subject written as JSON text, for a reader that does
      not read qlang: `{:a 1 :b 2} | json` answers `{"a":1,"b":2}`.
      Every value answers, and the kinds of qlang alone give way at
      this boundary: a keyword writes as its bare name, a key and a
      value alike; a set as an array; a value under a tag as an object
      of its `$tag` and its `payload`, an error at rest among them; a
      quote, a doc and a tag name as
      the string of their print. So `parseJson` reads a keyword back as
      its string and a set as a vector: the text that reads back as the
      value is `print`. The call takes no value, an indent among them.
      ::link~(::any/print | doc) ::link~(::string/parseJson | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ The JSON text of the subject. ~~| ::string
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `json` writes the whole subject on one line and takes no
            value, an indent among them: a field is written by reading it
            first, `get /a | json`.
        ~~|
    | proves :writesAMapAsAnObject
        |~~ A map is written as an object. ~~|
        ~({:a 1 :b 2} | json | eq "{\"a\":1,\"b\":2}")
    | proves :writesKeywordsAsNames
        |~~ A keyword is written as its bare name. ~~|
        ~([:foo :bar] | json | eq "[\"foo\",\"bar\"]")
    | proves :writesAnErrorAtRestUnderItsTag
        |~~ An error at rest is written as a value under its tag. ~~|
        ~(::error{:k 1} | json | parseJson | keys | eq #[:"$tag" :payload])
    | proves :writesASetAsAnArray
        |~~ A set is written as an array. ~~|
        ~(#[1 2 3] | json | eq "[1,2,3]")
    | proves :writesATaggedValueAsAnObject
        |~~ A value under a tag is written as an object of its tag and its
            payload.
        ~~|
        ~(::Box{:a 1} | json | eq "{\"$tag\":\"Box\",\"payload\":{\"a\":1}}")
    | proves :writesAQuoteAsItsPrint
        |~~ A quote is written as the string of its print. ~~|
        ~(~(add 1) | json | eq "\"~(add 1)\"")
    | proves :readsBackAsStringsAndVectors
        |~~ `parseJson` reads a keyword back as its string and a set as a
            vector.
        ~~|
        ~({:k :foo :s #[1 2]} | json | parseJson | eq {:k "foo" :s [1 2]})
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesAnIndent
        |~~ An indent given to `json` is refused under the verb. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | json 2 !| tag | eq ::any/json/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:any/print
  |~~ Answers the text that reads back as the subject, `42 | print`
      answering `"42"`, the text a host shows for an answer other than a
      string. An atom prints as its literal, a string inside its quotes;
      a vector and a map by their brackets around the print of each part;
      a set, a quote, a doc and an error by brackets of their own; any
      other value under a tag as its tag before the print of its payload.
      A set prints in its one order, and the text reads back as the value
      by `parse` and `open`. A kind may answer a `print` of its own
      declared under it, `:Box/print`, and the print of a value takes it
      for each part of that kind; a print the reader declares under a kind
      of the language, `:number/print`, answers the reader's own calls
      alone. The call takes no value: a print within a budget is
      `excerpt 80 | print`.
      ::link~(::any/json | doc) ::link~(::any/excerpt | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ The literal the subject prints as. ~~| ::string
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `print` writes the whole subject and takes no value: a print
            within a budget of characters is `excerpt 80 | print`.
        ~~|
    | proves :printsANumber
        |~~ A number prints as its literal. ~~|
        ~(42 | print | eq "42")
    | proves :printsAStringInsideItsQuotes
        |~~ A string prints inside its quotes. ~~|
        ~("x" | print | eq "\"x\"")
    | proves :printsATaggedValue
        |~~ A tagged value prints with its tag. ~~|
        ~(::builtin{:a 1} | print | eq "::builtin{:a 1}")
    | proves :printsASetInItsOrder
        |~~ A set prints in its one order. ~~|
        ~("#[2 1]" | parse | open | print | eq "#[1 2]")
    | proves :readsBack
        |~~ The text reads back as the value. ~~|
        ~({:a [1 "x"]} | :v it | print | parse | open | eq v)
    | proves :takesThePrintOfAKind
        |~~ The print of a value takes the `print` of a kind for each part
            of it.
        ~~|
        ~(:Box |~~ A box. ~~| | :Box/print ~("box") | [::Box[1] 2] | print | eq "[box 2]")
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesABudget
        |~~ A budget given to `print` is refused under the verb. ~~|
        ~([1 2] | print 80 !| tag | eq ::any/print/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:any/legend
  |~~ Answers the legend of the subject, a `::legend`, which a host prints
      as the print of the subject laid out by its kind, each part the kind
      reads by a key or an index of its payload on a line of its own beside
      the gloss of its page, and a part at an index beside its name too,
      which its print does not write: `::point[0 1 2] | legend` lays the
      line `1` out beside
      `|~ The line of the point, counted from one · line ~|`. The gloss is
      the words a page opens with, before the first colon, semicolon or
      period its prose ends a clause with, on one line; the head stands
      beside the gloss of the page `doc` reads of the subject; a part the
      kind computes, by code or a longer path, stands in no legend,
      `manifest` naming it and `doc` reading its page; and a part
      read by its name answers its own legend in turn,
      `"~{" | parse !| location | legend`. A value of a kind that wires
      nothing stands on one line beside its gloss, and a container that
      holds legends lays each on a line of its own. Every comment is
      closed, `|~ … ~|`, and whitespace, so the text read back is the
      subject, `print` answering the text and `payload` the subject.
      ::link~(::legend | doc) ::link~(::kind/wires | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ The legend of the subject. ~~| ::legend
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `legend` lays the whole subject out and takes no value: a part is
            read first by its name, `!| location | legend`.
        ~~|
    | proves :glossesTheHeadByThePageDocReads
        |~~ The head stands beside the gloss of the page `doc` reads of the
            subject.
        ~~|
        ~(::point[0 1 2] | legend | print | lines | first | contains "|~ A point is where a span of source text starts or ends ~|")
    | proves :glossesEachNumberOfASpanAtItsPlace
        |~~ A number of a span stands beside the gloss and the name of the
            part its index reads.
        ~~|
        ~(::span[0 1 1 4 1 5] | legend | print | lines | get /2 | contains "|~ The line of the start, counted from one · startLine ~|")
    | proves :readsBackAsTheSubject
        |~~ The print of the legend reads back as the value it tells. ~~|
        ~("~{" | parse !| :e it | legend | print | parse | open | eq e)
    | proves :standsOnOneLineForAKindThatWiresNothing
        |~~ A value of a kind that wires nothing stands on one line beside the
            gloss of its kind.
        ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | legend | print | lines | count | eq 1)
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesAValue
        |~~ A value given to `legend` is refused under the verb. ~~|
        ~([1 2] | legend 80 !| tag | eq ::any/legend/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | impl ~(tag ::legend)

:any/excerpt
  |~~ Answers the excerpt of the subject, a value of `::excerpt`, within
      the characters of print the call gives, three hundred where it gives
      none: the kind of the subject, its count and the parts that fit
      whole, taken from its two ends inward by turns, an end stopping at
      its first part that does not fit, a vector, a set, a quote and a doc
      under their indices and a map under its keys, and a string, a value
      with no parts and one its kind prints by the first and the last
      characters of their text under their offsets: `3410 | range | excerpt`
      answers `::excerpt{:of ::vec :count 3410 :parts {:"0" 0 …}}`. It
      always answers an excerpt, so the kind of its answer is known before
      the call, a value that fits shown whole in it, and its budget is no
      larger than the budget of every answer. A host tells so a fact of a
      failure past the room of a fact and an answer past the budget.
      ::link~(::excerpt | doc) ::link~(::any/print | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/AnswerBeyondBudgetError | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :budget
        |~~ The characters of the print of the excerpt, three hundred where
            the call gives none.
        ~~|
        ~(query /0 300) ::number
    | throws :budget/KindNotTakenError
        |~~ The budget is a number of characters, `excerpt 80`. ~~|
    | holds :budget/NotCountError
        |~~ The budget is a whole count of characters above zero,
            `excerpt 80`.
        ~~|
        ~(allOf (gt 0) ~(eq (floor)))
    | throws :budget/PastTheBudgetError
        |~~ An excerpt prints within the budget of every answer, which
            `:budget` names: a part of a value is read by `get`, `take` or
            `drop`, and the whole written by `print | out`.
        ~~|
        ~(wires :budget |~~ The budget of every answer. ~~| ::number
          | wires :actualTag |~~ The kind of the budget given. ~~| ::tag
          | wires :actualValue |~~ The budget given. ~~| ::number)
    | returns |~~ The excerpt of the subject within the budget. ~~| ::excerpt
    | proves :showsTheEnds
        |~~ An excerpt shows the parts at the two ends of a vector. ~~|
        ~(3410 | range | excerpt | parts | get [/0 /1 /3409] | eq [0 1 3409])
    | proves :countsTheSubject
        |~~ An excerpt counts the parts of the value it tells. ~~|
        ~(3410 | range | excerpt | count | eq 3410)
    | proves :fitsTheBudget
        |~~ The print of an excerpt fits its budget. ~~|
        ~(3410 | range | excerpt 120 | print | count | lte 120)
    | proves :showsAValueThatFitsWhole
        |~~ A value that fits answers an excerpt that shows all of it. ~~|
        ~([1 2] | excerpt | get [~(tag) ~(parts)] | eq [::excerpt {:"0" 1 :"1" 2}])
    | proves :showsAMapUnderItsKeys
        |~~ A map shows its entries under their keys. ~~|
        ~(100 | range | map ~(get [~(toString | toKeyword) ~(it)]) | toMap | excerpt | parts | keys | has :"0")
    | proves :showsTheEndsOfAString
        |~~ A string shows its first and its last characters under their
            offsets.
        ~~|
        ~(2000 | range | map ~("ab") | join "" | excerpt | get [~(of) ~(count) ~(parts | get /0 | startsWith "abab")] | eq [::string 4000 true])
    | proves :budget/KindNotTakenError/refusesAString
        |~~ A budget that is no number is refused under the input. ~~|
        ~([1 2] | excerpt "x" !| tag | eq ::any/excerpt/budget/KindNotTakenError)
    | proves :budget/NotCountError/refusesNoneAndAFraction
        |~~ A budget of no characters or of a fraction of one is refused.
        ~~|
        ~(get [~([1 2] | excerpt 0 !| tag) ~([1 2] | excerpt 2.5 !| tag)] | eq [::any/excerpt/budget/NotCountError ::any/excerpt/budget/NotCountError])
    | proves :budget/PastTheBudgetError/refusesABudgetPastTheBudgetOfEveryAnswer
        |~~ A budget past the budget of every answer is refused. ~~|
        ~([1 2] | excerpt 300000 !| get [~(tag) ~(budget)] | eq [::any/excerpt/budget/PastTheBudgetError 16000])
    | impl ::builtin{}

:any/if
  |~~ Answers the branch the condition chooses, run against the subject:
      `then` when the condition holds, `else` when it fails,
      `5 | if (gt 0) ~("positive") ~("negative")` answering `"positive"`.
      The condition is a boolean computed against the subject at the
      call, `if (get /age | gte 18)`; a branch is code, a quote, so only the
      chosen branch runs, and the empty quote `~()` leaves the subject
      as it is. So the condition is no quote, `if ~(gt 0)` being
      refused, and null and a number are refused too, having no second
      reading; a field the record lacks answers its error, which
      `query /flag | eq true` asks around. Under `map` the subject is
      each element, `map ~(if (gt 0) ~(it) ~(0))`. The call takes the
      condition and both branches, and a chain of conditions is `cond`.
      ::link~(::any/cond | doc) ::link~(::any/coalesce | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :condition
        |~~ The condition, a boolean computed against the subject at the
            call.
        ~~|
        /0 ::boolean
    | throws :condition/KindNotTakenError
        |~~ The condition is a boolean computed at the call, `if (gt 0)`:
            a quote, `~(gt 0)`, is code and no boolean, and null or a
            number has no second reading.
        ~~|
    | takes :then
        |~~ The branch run when the condition holds, a quote. ~~|
        /1 #[::path ::quote]
    | throws :then/KindNotTakenError
        |~~ The then branch is code, a quote, `~("yes")`, or a path, run
            only when chosen.
        ~~|
    | takes :else
        |~~ The branch run when the condition fails, a quote. ~~|
        /2 #[::path ::quote]
    | throws :else/KindNotTakenError
        |~~ The else branch is code, a quote, `~("no")`, or a path, run
            only when chosen.
        ~~|
    | throws :else/NotGivenError
        |~~ `if` takes both branches: a branch that leaves the subject as
            it is is the empty quote, `if (gt 0) ~(add 1) ~()`.
        ~~|
    | returns |~~ The answer of the branch the condition chose. ~~| ::any
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `if` takes one condition and two branches: a chain of
            conditions is `cond`,
            `cond ~(gte 90) ~("A") ~(gte 80) ~("B") ~("C")`.
        ~~|
    | proves :runsThenWhenItHolds
        |~~ The then branch runs when the condition holds. ~~|
        ~(5 | if (gt 0) ~("positive") ~("non-positive") | eq "positive")
    | proves :runsElseWhenItFails
        |~~ The else branch runs when it does not. ~~|
        ~(-1 | if (gt 0) ~("positive") ~("non-positive") | eq "non-positive")
    | proves :readsTheSubject
        |~~ The condition reads the subject. ~~|
        ~({:age 25} | if (get /age | gte 18) ~("adult") ~("minor") | eq "adult")
    | proves :runsTheBranchOnTheSubject
        |~~ A branch runs against the subject. ~~|
        ~(5 | if (gt 0) ~(add 100) ~() | eq 105)
    | proves :runsOnlyTheChosenBranch
        |~~ The branch not chosen does not run. ~~|
        ~(5 | if true ~(1) ~(add "x") | eq 1)
    | proves :leavesTheSubjectByAnEmptyBranch
        |~~ The empty quote leaves the subject as it is. ~~|
        ~(-5 | if (gt 0) ~(add 100) ~() | eq -5)
    | proves :answersTheErrorOfAMissingField
        |~~ A condition that reads a field the record lacks answers its
            error.
        ~~|
        ~({} | if (get /flag) ~(1) ~(2) !| tag | eq ::path/KeyNotInMapError)
    | proves :asksAMissingFieldByQuery
        |~~ A field that may be missing is asked by `query`. ~~|
        ~({} | if (query /flag | eq true) ~(1) ~(2) | eq 2)
    | proves :choosesForEachElement
        |~~ Under `map` the subject is each element. ~~|
        ~([1 -2 3] | map ~(if (gt 0) ~(it) ~(0)) | eq [1 0 3])
    | proves :condition/KindNotTakenError/refusesAQuote
        |~~ A condition given as a quote is code and no boolean. ~~|
        ~(5 | if ~(gt 0) ~(1) ~(2)
          !| get [~(tag) ~(actualTag)]
          |  eq [::any/if/condition/KindNotTakenError ::quote])
    | proves :condition/KindNotTakenError/refusesNull
        |~~ A null condition is refused. ~~|
        ~({:flag null} | if (get /flag) ~(1) ~(2)
          !| get [~(tag) ~(actualTag)]
          |  eq [::any/if/condition/KindNotTakenError ::null])
    | proves :condition/KindNotTakenError/refusesANumber
        |~~ A number condition is refused. ~~|
        ~(5 | if 1 ~("yes") ~("no")
          !| get [~(tag) ~(actualTag)]
          |  eq [::any/if/condition/KindNotTakenError ::number])
    | proves :then/KindNotTakenError/refusesAString
        |~~ A string given as the then branch is refused. ~~|
        ~(5 | if true "yes" ~("no")
          !| get [~(tag) ~(actualTag)]
          |  eq [::any/if/then/KindNotTakenError ::string])
    | proves :else/KindNotTakenError/refusesAString
        |~~ A string given as the else branch is refused. ~~|
        ~(5 | if true ~("yes") "no"
          !| get [~(tag) ~(actualTag)]
          |  eq [::any/if/else/KindNotTakenError ::string])
    | proves :else/NotGivenError/refusesACallWithoutElse
        |~~ A call without the else branch is refused under the input. ~~|
        ~(5 | if (gt 0) ~(add 1) !| tag | eq ::any/if/else/NotGivenError)
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesAThirdBranch
        |~~ A third branch is refused under the verb. ~~|
        ~(5 | if true ~(1) ~(2) ~(3) !| tag | eq ::any/if/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:any/coalesce
  |~~ Answers the first of the alternatives the call gives that answers a
      value, each in its turn: code, a quote or a path, runs against the
      subject, and a value answers itself,
      `{:nickname null :name "alice"} | coalesce /nickname /name`
      answering `"alice"`. An alternative passes to the next where it
      answers null, or the refusal of a read `query` answers null for met
      on its own way, a key the map lacks, an index past the end or null on
      the way, so `coalesce /key ~(fallback)` reads "the key where the map
      holds it, else the fallback"; a miss in the code a verb ran on a part
      of it, `map /price`, and any other failure of its code is the answer
      of the call, a misspelled name among them, and every other value ends
      the walk, false, `0` and `""` among them. When every alternative
      passes the call answers null. A value alone stands after the subject,
      `coalesce 30` reading "the subject, else 30", and several values
      alone are refused, the subject first written by a quote,
      `coalesce ~(it) e 8080`, and a field by a path, `coalesce /t 30`. An
      error the steps before answered passes over the call, as over every
      step, so a field that may be missing is read inside an alternative,
      `coalesce /key 0`, not before it. The call takes one alternative at
      least.
      ::link~(::any/if | doc) ::link~(::any/cond | doc)
      ::link~(::any/query | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :alternatives
        |~~ The alternatives, in their order, each code, a quote or a path,
            run against the subject, or a value that answers itself.
        ~~|
        ~(it)
    | holds :alternatives/EmptyError
        |~~ `coalesce` answers the first of its alternatives that answers
            a value, so it takes one at least,
            `coalesce /nickname ~("anonymous")`.
        ~~|
        ~(count | gt 0)
    | holds :alternatives/ValuesAloneError
        |~~ `coalesce` reads the subject before one value,
            `query /t | coalesce 30`, and several values alone read no
            subject: the subject first is written by a quote,
            `coalesce ~(it) e 8080`, a value a name holds by a quote,
            `coalesce ~(e) 8080`, and a field by a path, `coalesce /t 30`.
        ~~|
        ~(map ~tag | anyOf ~(count | lt 2) ~(has ::quote) ~(has ::path))
    | returns
        |~~ The first answer that is a value, a failure of the code that
            answered it, or null when every alternative passes.
        ~~|
        ::any
    | proves :passesOverNull
        |~~ A null answer passes to the next alternative. ~~|
        ~({:nickname null :name "alice"} | coalesce /nickname /name | eq "alice")
    | proves :answersTheFirstValue
        |~~ The first answer that is a value wins. ~~|
        ~({:nickname "ace" :name "alice"} | coalesce /nickname /name | eq "ace")
    | proves :keepsFalse
        |~~ False ends the walk as a value. ~~|
        ~({:on false} | coalesce /on ~(true) | not)
    | proves :answersNullWhenEveryAlternativePasses
        |~~ When every alternative answers null or misses its part, the
            call answers null.
        ~~|
        ~({} | coalesce /a /b | eq null)
    | proves :passesOverAMissedPart
        |~~ A key the map lacks passes to the next alternative. ~~|
        ~({:a 2 :b 3} | coalesce /missing ~("fallback") | eq "fallback")
    | proves :passesOverNullOnTheWay
        |~~ A null on the way of a path passes, as `query` reads it. ~~|
        ~({:user null} | coalesce /user/name ~("anonymous") | eq "anonymous")
    | proves :answersAnyOtherFailureOfItsCode
        |~~ Code that fails otherwise than by a missed part answers the
            call with its failure.
        ~~|
        ~({} | coalesce ~(nosuchword) 30 !| tag | eq ::call/UnknownNameError)
    | proves :answersAMissInCodeRunOnAPart
        |~~ A field one element lacks under `map` is the answer of the
            call.
        ~~|
        ~({:items [{:price 1} {:cost 2}]} | coalesce ~(get /items | map /price) [] !| tag | eq ::path/KeyNotInMapError)
    | proves :readsTheSubjectFirstByAQuote
        |~~ A quote of `it` stands the subject first among several values.
        ~~|
        ~(9000 | coalesce ~(it) 7000 8080 | eq 9000)
    | proves :fallsToTheLast
        |~~ The last alternative answers when the rest give nothing. ~~|
        ~({:nickname null :name null}
          | coalesce /nickname /name ~("anonymous") | eq "anonymous")
    | proves :takesAValueForAnAlternative
        |~~ A value stands for an alternative as its own answer. ~~|
        ~({:a 2} | coalesce /missing "fallback" | eq "fallback")
    | proves :defaultsAMissingField
        |~~ A field missing or null takes the default. ~~|
        ~([{:t 45} {:t null} {}] | map ~(query /t | coalesce 30) | eq [45 30 30])
    | proves :keepsAValue
        |~~ A value present stays. ~~|
        ~({:tags ["a"]} | get /tags | coalesce [] | eq ["a"])
    | proves :passesAnErrorBeforeIt
        |~~ An error the steps before answered passes over the call. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | get /missing | coalesce 0 !| tag | eq ::path/KeyNotInMapError)
    | proves :alternatives/EmptyError/refusesNoAlternative
        |~~ A coalesce of no alternative is refused. ~~|
        ~({} | coalesce
          !| get [~(tag) ~(actualValue)]
          |  eq [::any/coalesce/alternatives/EmptyError ::any/coalesce[]])
    | proves :alternatives/ValuesAloneError/refusesSeveralValues
        |~~ Several values alone are refused. ~~|
        ~({:t null} | coalesce (get /t) 30 !| tag | eq ::any/coalesce/alternatives/ValuesAloneError)
    | proves :keepsZeroAndTheEmptyString
        |~~ Zero and the empty string end the walk. ~~|
        ~([0 ""] | map ~(coalesce 1) | eq [0 ""])
    | impl ::builtin{}

:any/cond
  |~~ Answers the branch of the first predicate that holds of the subject,
      the clauses alternating predicate and branch, each a quote run
      against the subject in its turn:
      `85 | cond ~(gte 90) ~("A") ~(gte 80) ~("B") ~("F")` answers `"B"`,
      so a chain of nested `if` flattens into one call. A clause left over
      after the pairs is the default branch; without one, a walk that
      matches nothing answers null. Each predicate answers a boolean, and
      one that answers another kind is refused at its index. Unlike the
      condition of `if`, a predicate is code, a quote, and one computed at
      the call, `(gte 90)`, is refused at its index. The call takes one
      pair at least.
      ::link~(::any/if | doc) ::link~(::any/coalesce | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :clauses
        |~~ The clauses, each a quote: predicates and their branches in
            turn, a last one alone the default.
        ~~|
        ~(it) [#[::path ::quote]]
    | throws :clauses/ElementKindNotTakenError
        |~~ Each of the clauses is code, a quote, `~(gte 90)`: a predicate
            runs against the subject in its turn, so one computed at the
            call is refused at its index.
        ~~|
    | holds :clauses/NoBranchesError
        |~~ `cond` takes its clauses in pairs, a predicate and its branch,
            a last clause alone the default; a call without one pair, a
            lone default among them, has nothing to answer.
        ~~|
        ~(count | gte 2)
    | returns
        |~~ The answer of the branch chosen, null when no predicate holds
            and no default is given.
        ~~|
        ::any
    | throws :ConditionKindNotTakenError
        |~~ Every predicate of `cond` answers a boolean; `:index` names
            the predicate that answered another kind by its index among the
            args, from zero.
        ~~|
        ::fn/ElementKindNotTakenError
    | proves :picksTheFirstThatHolds
        |~~ Eighty-five is a B. ~~|
        ~(85 | cond ~(gte 90) ~("A") ~(gte 80) ~("B") ~(gte 70) ~("C") ~("F") | eq "B")
    | proves :readsTheSubject
        |~~ The predicates read the subject. ~~|
        ~({:role "admin"}
          | cond ~(get /role | eq "admin") ~("full") ~(get /role | eq "user") ~("limited")
          | eq "full")
    | proves :fallsToTheDefault
        |~~ A score no predicate holds of takes the default. ~~|
        ~(65 | cond ~(gte 90) ~("A") ~(gte 80) ~("B") ~(gte 70) ~("C") ~("F") | eq "F")
    | proves :answersNullWithoutADefault
        |~~ A walk that matches nothing without a default answers null. ~~|
        ~(5 | cond ~(gt 9) ~("big") | eq null)
    | proves :clauses/ElementKindNotTakenError/refusesAComputedPredicate
        |~~ A predicate computed at the call is refused at its index. ~~|
        ~(5 | cond (gt 3) ~("big")
          !| get [~(tag) ~(index)]
          |  eq [::any/cond/clauses/ElementKindNotTakenError 0])
    | proves :clauses/NoBranchesError/refusesNoPair
        |~~ A cond of no pair is refused. ~~|
        ~(5 | cond !| tag | eq ::any/cond/clauses/NoBranchesError)
    | proves :clauses/NoBranchesError/refusesALoneDefault
        |~~ A lone default is no pair. ~~|
        ~(5 | cond ~("only") !| tag | eq ::any/cond/clauses/NoBranchesError)
    | proves :ConditionKindNotTakenError/refusesANumberAnswer
        |~~ A predicate that answers a number is refused at its index. ~~|
        ~(5 | cond ~(1) ~("yes")
          !| get [~(tag) ~(index) ~(actualTag) ~(expected)]
          |  eq [::any/cond/ConditionKindNotTakenError 0 ::number #[::boolean]])
    | impl ::builtin{}

:any/apply
  |~~ Answers the code the call gives run against the subject,
      `5 | apply ~(add 1)` answering `6`. The code is a quote, a path or
      a function, or a name that holds one, `apply double`, and a
      function takes its args as a vector, as a call holds its args,
      `5 | apply f [3]`. Its steps run in order, a
      leading `!|` routing the first through the fail track, so the steps
      a frame of a trace skipped run again, as held code runs. Code held
      as the subject runs on itself by `apply it`, and text runs on a
      subject once `parse` reads it, `5 | apply ("add 3" | parse)`. The
      code runs under the fork rule: its declarations stay inside it, and
      only its value comes out. A step written without its quote runs at the
      call, so `apply (add 1)` is refused, as is the error a step
      answered there. The call takes one code; code of several steps is
      one quote.
      ::link~(::quote | doc) ::link~(::fn | doc) ::link~(::string/parse | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/code | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :code
        |~~ The code the call runs, a quote, a path or a function, or a
            name that holds one.
        ~~|
        /0 #[::fn ::path ::quote]
    | throws :code/KindNotTakenError
        |~~ The code is a quote, `apply ~(add 1)`, a path, `apply /a`, or
            a function: a step written without its quote runs at the call,
            and its answer, an error among them, is no code.
        ~~|
    | throws :code/NotGivenError
        |~~ `apply` runs the code the call gives, `apply ~(add 1)`; code
            held as the subject runs on itself by `apply it`.
        ~~|
    | takes :args
        |~~ The args of a function, a vector, as a call holds its args.
        ~~|
        ~(query /1 []) ::vec
    | throws :args/KindNotTakenError
        |~~ The args of a function are a vector, `apply f [3]`, as a call
            holds its args.
        ~~|
    | throws :args/NotTakenError
        |~~ A quote and a path take no args: steps run in order inside
            one quote, `apply ~(add 1 | mul 2)`, and args go to a
            function, `apply f [3]`.
        ~~|
        ::fn/InvariantNotHeldError
    | returns |~~ The answer of the code run against the subject. ~~| ::any
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `apply` takes the code and the args of a function, one
            vector, `apply f [3]`.
        ~~|
    | proves :runsTheCode
        |~~ The code runs against the subject. ~~|
        ~(5 | apply ~(add 1) | eq 6)
    | proves :runsANameThatHoldsCode
        |~~ A name that holds a quote runs as the quote. ~~|
        ~(:double ~(mul 2) | 5 | apply double | eq 10)
    | proves :runsStepsInOrder
        |~~ Several steps run in order. ~~|
        ~([[1 2] [3 4]] | apply ~(flat | sort) | eq [1 2 3 4])
    | proves :runsParsedText
        |~~ Text parsed into a quote runs on a subject. ~~|
        ~(5 | apply ("add 3" | parse) | eq 8)
    | proves :keepsTheDeclarationsInside
        |~~ The declarations of the code stay inside it. ~~|
        ~(5 | apply ~(:x 1 | x) | :y it | env | has :x | not)
    | proves :code/KindNotTakenError/refusesANumber
        |~~ A number computed at the call is refused. ~~|
        ~(42 | apply 1 !| get [~(tag) ~(actualTag)] | eq [::any/apply/code/KindNotTakenError ::number])
    | proves :code/KindNotTakenError/refusesTheErrorOfAStep
        |~~ The error a step answered at the call is refused as no code. ~~|
        ~(5 | apply (get /nope)
          !| get [~(tag) ~(actualTag)]
          |  eq [::any/apply/code/KindNotTakenError ::path/SubjectHasNoPartsError])
    | proves :code/NotGivenError/refusesACallWithNoCode
        |~~ A call with no code is refused under the input. ~~|
        ~(~(add 1) | apply !| tag | eq ::any/apply/code/NotGivenError)
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesAThirdArg
        |~~ A call with an arg after the args of the function is refused
            under the verb.
        ~~|
        ~(5 | apply ~(add 1) [] [] !| tag | eq ::any/apply/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | proves :args/NotTakenError/refusesTheArgsOfAQuote
        |~~ A second quote is no arg of the first. ~~|
        ~(5 | apply ~(add 1) ~(mul 2) !| tag | eq ::any/apply/args/NotTakenError)
    | proves :args/KindNotTakenError/refusesANumber
        |~~ A number is no vector of args. ~~|
        ~(5 | apply ~(add 1) 3 !| tag | eq ::any/apply/args/KindNotTakenError)
    | proves :runsAFunctionOnItsArgs
        |~~ A function runs on the subject with its args. ~~|
        ~(5 | apply (::fn | takes :by |~~ How much. ~~| /0 | impl ~(add by)) [3] | eq 8)
    | proves :passesAValueByALeadingFailStep
        |~~ A leading `!|` passes a value on. ~~|
        ~(5 | apply ~(!| 0) | eq 5)
    | proves :runsARecordedSuffixAgain
        |~~ The steps a trace skipped run again on a value. ~~|
        ~(1 | add "x" | mul 2 | add 3 !| (:rest (trace | first | skipped) | 5 | apply rest) | eq 13)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:any/env
  |~~ Answers what the reader declared before the step, whatever the
      subject, the cells of a session before it among them: a map from
      each name to the record of its binding, `::binding` with its name,
      its docs, its value, its source and its origin. `:limit 10 | env`
      answers the record of `:limit`, and `env | get /limit` hands it to the
      next step. A name with a path is held by its address. Inside a
      fork it holds the names before the fork and the fork's own, and
      the fork's stay inside it; code held as data holds its own and the
      nodes and the labels of the step it came from, a value the reader
      named withheld from it. What the language and the host declared
      stands apart, so at the start of a query it answers `{}`: `kinds`
      lists their kinds, and `manifest` what lies under a noun. The call
      takes no name.
      ::link~(::any/binding | doc) ::link~(::any/kinds | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns
        |~~ The records of what the reader declared, by name. ~~|
        ::map
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `env` answers every name the reader declared and takes none:
            one record is read by its key, `env | get /limit`.
        ~~|
    | proves :holdsADeclaredName
        |~~ A name declared before the step is held. ~~|
        ~(:limit 10 | env | has :limit)
    | proves :answersWhateverTheSubject
        |~~ The subject leaves the answer as it is. ~~|
        ~(:limit 10 | [1 2] | env | has :limit)
    | proves :holdsTheRecord
        |~~ Each name holds the record its declaration wrote. ~~|
        ~(:limit 10 | env | get /limit | value | eq 10)
    | proves :holdsANameWithAPath
        |~~ A name with a path is held by its address. ~~|
        ~(:Box |~~ A box. ~~| | :Box/area ~(1) | env | keys | eq #[:Box :Box/area])
    | proves :holdsTheNamesOfAFork
        |~~ Inside a fork the names before it and its own are held. ~~|
        ~(:a 1 | (:b 2 | env | keys) | eq #[:a :b])
    | proves :keepsTheNamesOfAForkInside
        |~~ The names of a fork stay inside it. ~~|
        ~(:a 1 | (:b 2) | env | keys | eq #[:a])
    | proves :isEmptyAtTheStart
        |~~ Nothing is declared at the start. ~~|
        ~(env | eq {})
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesAName
        |~~ A name given to `env` is refused under the verb. ~~|
        ~(:limit 10 | env :limit !| tag | eq ::any/env/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:any/kinds
  |~~ Answers the set of the labels the scope reads, the kinds declared
      under a name, each by its tag name, whatever the subject: those the
      language and the host declared, the topic of the language among
      them, and those the reader declares. A query asks which kinds there
      are before it asks one of them for its page, `::set | doc`, or for
      what lies under it, `::set | manifest`. A chapter a topic tells is no
      kind: `::qlang/values` is a page `doc` reads, which `manifest` of its
      topic lists. The call takes no kind.
      ::link~(::any/env | doc) ::link~(::qlang | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ The tag names of the kinds the scope reads. ~~| ::set
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `kinds` answers every kind and takes none: whether a kind is
            there is `kinds | has ::set`.
        ~~|
    | proves :holdsTheKindsOfTheLanguage
        |~~ The kinds of the language are read. ~~|
        ~(kinds | has ::vec)
    | proves :answersWhateverTheSubject
        |~~ The subject leaves the answer as it is. ~~|
        ~("text" | kinds | has ::vec)
    | proves :holdsTheTopic
        |~~ The topic of the language is a label too. ~~|
        ~(kinds | has ::qlang)
    | proves :holdsAReadersKind
        |~~ A kind the reader declares is read. ~~|
        ~(:Box |~~ A box. ~~| | kinds | has ::Box)
    | proves :holdsNoChapter
        |~~ A chapter of the topic is no kind. ~~|
        ~(kinds | has ::qlang/values | not)
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesAKind
        |~~ A kind given to `kinds` is refused under the verb. ~~|
        ~(kinds ::set !| tag | eq ::any/kinds/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:any/binding
  |~~ Answers the record of a name, what its declaration wrote into its
      scope: `::binding` with its `:name`, its `:docs`, its `:value`, its
      `:source` and its `:origin`, the source it was read from, so where a
      name was declared is read as data,
      `::vec/filter | binding | origin` answering `:qlang/vec`. A
      verb by its address reads the record its source declared, a name of
      the scope the record its declaration wrote, and any other value the
      record of its kind. The anchor, a keyword after it, reads the record
      of the node the subject holds under it, an input of a verb or the
      verb a value calls. `doc` reads the page of the record, and
      `source` every declaration written under the name's address, the
      record's `:source` holding the step of the declaration that wrote
      it. A name nothing declares is refused, with
      the addresses where a verb of that name lives.
      ::link~(::any/doc | doc) ::link~(::any/source | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :anchor
        |~~ The last name of the node read under the subject, a keyword,
            null when the call gives none.
        ~~|
        ~(query /0) #[::keyword ::null]
    | throws :anchor/KindNotTakenError
        |~~ The anchor is a keyword or null, the last name of a node under
            the subject, `::vec | binding :sort`.
        ~~|
    | returns |~~ The record of the name. ~~| ::binding
    | throws :UnknownNameError
        |~~ `binding` found no declaration of the name it read: a keyword
            that names no binding of its scope, the last name of a verb
            declared under a kind among them, or a tag name that names
            nothing. `:name` holds the name, and `:addresses` the
            addresses of the pages of that name, one for each kind that
            answers it, so the verb is read by one of them,
            `::vec/filter | binding`. `source`, `doc`, `laws` and
            `runLaws` read a name as `binding` does, and each is
            refused under a tag of its own, so the tag says which of them
            read it. `:nearest` holds the names near the one it missed,
            the tags as deep as a tag it read, and for an anchor the subject
            holds no node under the nodes it holds and the verbs its walk
            reaches.
        ~~|
        ::any/UnknownNameError
    | proves :answersABinding
        |~~ The record is a `::binding`. ~~|
        ~(::vec/filter | binding | tag | eq ::binding)
    | proves :readsTheOriginOfAVerb
        |~~ A verb's record names the source that declared it. ~~|
        ~(::vec/filter | binding | origin | eq :qlang/vec)
    | proves :readsTheValueOfAName
        |~~ A name's record holds its value. ~~|
        ~(:x 1 | ::x | binding | value | eq 1)
    | proves :readsTheKindOfAValue
        |~~ A number reads the record of `::number`. ~~|
        ~(5 | binding | name | eq :number)
    | proves :readsAnInputByAnAnchor
        |~~ An anchor reads the record of an input of a verb. ~~|
        ~(::vec/take | binding :count | name | eq :vec/take/count)
    | proves :readsTheVerbAValueCalls
        |~~ An anchor on a value reads the record of the verb it calls. ~~|
        ~([1] | binding :count | name | eq :vec/count)
    | proves :holdsThePageDocReads
        |~~ The record holds the page `doc` reads. ~~|
        ~(:x |~~ The x. ~~| 1 | ::x | binding | docs | first | eq (::x | doc))
    | proves :anchor/KindNotTakenError/refusesANumber
        |~~ An anchor that is a number is refused under the input. ~~|
        ~(::vec/take | binding 1 !| tag | eq ::any/binding/anchor/KindNotTakenError)
    | proves :UnknownNameError/refusesANameNothingDeclares
        |~~ A name nothing declares is refused. ~~|
        ~(::nonexistent-binding | binding
          !| get [~(tag) ~(name)]
          |  eq [::any/binding/UnknownNameError ::nonexistent-binding])
    | proves :UnknownNameError/namesWhereItsVerbsLive
        |~~ The name of a verb is refused with the addresses where the
            verbs of that name live.
        ~~|
        ~(::filter | binding !| addresses | has ::vec/filter)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:any/source
  |~~ Answers the code written under a name, one quote of its
      declarations in the order they were written, which `toString`
      writes as its text, a declaration a line: the declaration that binds the
      name in its scope, the chain of a verb's function with its inputs,
      its answer, its refusals and its laws, and every declaration under
      its address. `::Tag | source` reads what was written
      under the tag, and any other value the declaration of its kind. A
      binding no declaration wrote, a node a chain lays among them, a
      refusal among those, holds no source, which `source` answers null
      for. The anchor, a keyword after it, reads the code of the node the
      subject holds under it, an input of a verb or the verb a value
      calls. A name nothing declares is refused, with the addresses where
      a verb of that name lives.
      ::link~(::any/doc | doc) ::link~(::any/binding | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :anchor
        |~~ The last name of the node read under the subject, a keyword,
            null when the call gives none.
        ~~|
        ~(query /0) #[::keyword ::null]
    | throws :anchor/KindNotTakenError
        |~~ The anchor is a keyword or null, the last name of a node under
            the subject, `::vec | source :sort`.
        ~~|
    | returns
        |~~ The code written under the name, null for a binding no
            declaration wrote.
        ~~|
        #[::quote ::null]
    | throws :UnknownNameError
        |~~ `source` found no declaration of the name it read, as
            `binding` finds none.
            ::link~(::any/binding/UnknownNameError | doc)
        ~~|
        ::any/UnknownNameError
    | proves :readsEveryDeclarationUnderAName
        |~~ The source of a verb is its declaration, the chain that holds
            its inputs, its refusals and its laws.
        ~~|
        ~(::vec/take | source | map /name | eq [:vec/take])
    | proves :readsInTheOrderWritten
        |~~ The declarations under a name read in the order they were
            written.
        ~~|
        ~(:Box |~~ A box. ~~| | :Box/a ~(get /0) | :Box/b ~(get /1) | :Box/note |~~ A note. ~~|
          | ::Box | source | map /name
          | eq [:Box :Box/a :Box/b :Box/note])
    | proves :printsTheDeclaration
        |~~ The source of a verb prints as its declaration. ~~|
        ~(::vec/filter | source | toString | contains ":vec/filter")
    | proves :printsItsNameAboveItsPage
        |~~ The source of a verb prints its name on a line of its own and
            its page under it, within the width of a line.
        ~~|
        ~(::number/round | source | toString | lines | take 2
          | eq [":number/round" "  |~~ Answers the subject rounded to the digits after the point the call"])
    | proves :printsARefusalInTheChainOfItsKind
        |~~ A refusal a kind pages prints in the source of the kind, the
            chain that declares it.
        ~~|
        ~(::number | source | toString | contains "throws :NotFiniteError")
    | proves :readsTheKindOfAValue
        |~~ A string reads the source of `::string`. ~~|
        ~("text" | source | eq (::string | source))
    | proves :readsAVerbByAnAnchor
        |~~ An anchor after a kind reads the source of its verb. ~~|
        ~(::vec | source :take | eq (::vec/take | source))
    | proves :readsTheVerbAValueCalls
        |~~ An anchor on a value reads the source of the verb it calls. ~~|
        ~([1] | source :count | eq (::vec/count | source))
    | proves :answersNullForANodeAChainLays
        |~~ A binding no declaration wrote, a node a chain lays, answers no
            source.
        ~~|
        ~(::number/add/addend | source | eq null)
    | proves :anchor/KindNotTakenError/refusesANumber
        |~~ An anchor that is a number is refused under the input. ~~|
        ~(::vec/take | source 1 !| tag | eq ::any/source/anchor/KindNotTakenError)
    | proves :UnknownNameError/refusesANameNothingDeclares
        |~~ A name nothing declares is refused. ~~|
        ~(::nonexistent-binding | source
          !| get [~(tag) ~(name)]
          |  eq [::any/source/UnknownNameError ::nonexistent-binding])
    | proves :UnknownNameError/namesWhereItsVerbsLive
        |~~ The name of a verb is refused with the addresses where the
            verbs of that name live.
        ~~|
        ~(::filter | source !| addresses | has ::vec/filter)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:any/doc
  |~~ Answers the page of a name, the passage from an address into its
      description: a name is read by its tag, `::vec/filter | doc` the
      page of a verb, `::limit | doc` the page of the declaration that
      binds `limit` in the scope, null for a declaration without one. A
      page answers its text by `content`, its links by `links` and its
      snippets by `snippets`, and the laws of the name stand under it,
      `::vec/take | laws`. Any other value reads the page of its kind, a
      keyword among them, `:limit | doc` the page of `::keyword`. The
      anchor, a keyword after `doc`, reads the page of the node the
      subject holds under it, the verb a value calls or an input of a
      verb; a vector of anchors reads them in turn, each under the node
      the one before reached, and a set of anchors reads a page at each.
      A name nothing declares is refused, with the addresses where a verb
      of that name lives, as is an anchor no node answers.
      ::link~(::any/laws | doc) ::link~(::doc | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :anchor
        |~~ The last name of the node read under the subject, a keyword, a
            vector of them read in turn or a set of anchors, null when the
            call gives none.
        ~~|
        ~(query /0) #[::keyword ::null ::set ::vec]
    | throws :anchor/KindNotTakenError
        |~~ The anchor is a keyword, a vector, a set or null, the last
            names of nodes under the subject, `::qlang | doc :pipeline`.
        ~~|
    | throws :anchor/NameKindNotTakenError
        |~~ A name `doc` reads in turn is a keyword, each naming a node
            under the one before it, `doc [:take :count]`.
        ~~|
        ::fn/ElementKindNotTakenError
    | throws :anchor/ElementKindNotTakenError
        |~~ An element of a set of anchors is a keyword, a vector of names
            read in turn or null, each reading a page,
            `doc #[:take [:take :count]]`.
        ~~|
        ::fn/ElementKindNotTakenError
    | returns
        |~~ The page of the name, null for a declaration without one, a
            vector of pages for a set of anchors.
        ~~|
        #[::doc ::null ::vec]
    | throws :UnknownNameError
        |~~ `doc` found no declaration of the name it read, as `binding`
            finds none, or no node under the one before it at an anchor it
            read in turn.
            ::link~(::any/binding/UnknownNameError | doc)
        ~~|
        ::any/UnknownNameError
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `doc` reads one anchor: anchors read in turn are a vector,
            `doc [:take :count]`, and the pages of several a set,
            `doc #[:take :drop]`.
        ~~|
    | proves :readsThePageOfAVerb
        |~~ The page of `filter` speaks of its predicate. ~~|
        ~(::vec/filter | doc | content | contains "predicate")
    | proves :answersNullForADeclarationWithoutAPage
        |~~ A declaration without a page answers null. ~~|
        ~(:x 1 | ::x | doc | eq null)
    | proves :readsThePageOfARefusal
        |~~ A refusal's page is a doc. ~~|
        ~(::number/add/SubjectNotNumberError | doc | tag | eq ::doc)
    | proves :readsThePageOfAKind
        |~~ A number reads the page of `::number`. ~~|
        ~(5 | doc | eq (::number | doc))
    | proves :readsAKeywordByItsKind
        |~~ A keyword is a value and names nothing: it reads the page of
            `::keyword`, and the declaration it spells is read by its tag.
        ~~|
        ~(:limit |~~ The cap. ~~| 5
          | get [~(:limit | doc) ~(::limit | doc | content)]
          | eq (get [~(::keyword | doc) "The cap."]))
    | proves :readsANodeByAnAnchor
        |~~ An anchor reads the page of a node. ~~|
        ~(::map | doc :minus | eq (::map/minus | doc))
    | proves :readsTheVerbAValueCalls
        |~~ An anchor on a value reads the page of the verb it calls. ~~|
        ~([1 2] | doc :count | eq (::vec/count | doc))
    | proves :readsAnInputOfAVerb
        |~~ An anchor reads the page of an input of a verb. ~~|
        ~(::number/add | doc :addend | eq (::number/add/addend | doc))
    | proves :readsAnchorsInTurn
        |~~ A vector of anchors reads them in turn. ~~|
        ~(::vec | doc [:take :count] | eq (::vec/take/count | doc))
    | proves :readsAPageAtEachAnchor
        |~~ A set of anchors reads a page at each. ~~|
        ~(::qlang | doc #[:pipeline :errors] | count | eq 2)
    | proves :anchor/KindNotTakenError/refusesANumber
        |~~ An anchor that is a number is refused under the input. ~~|
        ~(::vec | doc 5 !| tag | eq ::any/doc/anchor/KindNotTakenError)
    | proves :UnknownNameError/refusesANameNothingDeclares
        |~~ A name nothing declares is refused. ~~|
        ~(::nonexistent-binding | doc
          !| get [~(tag) ~(name)]
          |  eq [::any/doc/UnknownNameError ::nonexistent-binding])
    | proves :UnknownNameError/namesWhereItsVerbsLive
        |~~ The name of a verb is refused with the addresses where the
            verbs of that name live.
        ~~|
        ~(::filter | doc !| addresses | has ::vec/filter)
    | proves :UnknownNameError/namesWhereTheVerbsOfAnotherNameLive
        |~~ Another name of several verbs is refused with their addresses. ~~|
        ~(::reduce | doc !| addresses | has ::vec/reduce)
    | proves :UnknownNameError/refusesAnAnchorNoNodeAnswers
        |~~ An anchor no node answers is refused with the name it read. ~~|
        ~(::vec/take | doc :nope !| get [~(tag) ~(name)] | eq [::any/doc/UnknownNameError :nope])
    | proves :UnknownNameError/namesTheNodesNear
        |~~ An anchor the subject holds no node under names the nodes near
            it.
        ~~|
        ~(::qlang | doc :pipelin !| nearest | eq [:pipeline])
    | proves :anchor/NameKindNotTakenError/refusesANumberAmongTheNames
        |~~ A number among the names read in turn is refused at its index.
        ~~|
        ~(::vec | doc [:take 1] !| get [~(tag) ~(index) ~(expected)] | eq [::any/doc/anchor/NameKindNotTakenError 1 #[::keyword]])
    | proves :anchor/ElementKindNotTakenError/refusesANumberInASet
        |~~ A number in a set of anchors is refused at its index in the set.
        ~~|
        ~(::vec | doc #[:take 5] !| get [~(tag) ~(index) ~(expected)] | eq [::any/doc/anchor/ElementKindNotTakenError 0 #[::keyword ::null ::vec]])
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesTwoAnchors
        |~~ Two anchors given apart are refused under the verb. ~~|
        ~(::vec | doc :take :count !| tag | eq ::any/doc/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:any/manifest
  |~~ Answers the set of the nodes declared one name under the name the
      subject reads, as `doc` reads it, each by its address:
      `::number | manifest` the verbs and the refusals of numbers,
      `::vec/take | manifest` the inputs and the answer of `take`,
      `::qlang | manifest` the chapters of the language and the refusal of
      an answer past the budget, and a value the
      names under the kind it shows, `5 | manifest` those of numbers. A
      name nothing declares is refused under the verb,
      `::any/manifest/UnknownNameError`. The laws of a
      name are no part of the answer, `laws` reads them; the kinds of the
      scope `kinds` lists, and what the reader declared `env`; and what
      one name holds the axes read, `::vec/count | source`, `| doc`. An
      anchor, a keyword after it, reads the node the name holds under it,
      as `doc` reads one, `::vec | manifest :take`; the noun itself is the
      subject, so `manifest ::vec` is refused.
      ::snippet~(::number | manifest) ::link~(::any/laws | doc)
      ::link~(::any/kinds | doc) ::link~(::any/env | doc)
      ::link~(::any/source | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :anchor
        |~~ The last name of the node whose names the call answers, a
            keyword, null when the call gives none.
        ~~|
        ~(query /0) #[::keyword ::null]
    | throws :anchor/KindNotTakenError
        |~~ The anchor is a keyword or null, the last name of a node under
            the subject, `::vec | manifest :take`; a noun is the subject,
            `::vec | manifest`.
        ~~|
    | returns
        |~~ The addresses of the names declared one name under the name. ~~|
        ::set
    | throws :UnknownNameError
        |~~ `manifest` found no declaration of the name it read, as
            `binding` finds none.
            ::link~(::any/binding/UnknownNameError | doc)
        ~~|
        ::any/UnknownNameError
    | proves :holdsAVerbOfTheNoun
        |~~ The manifest of numbers holds `add`. ~~|
        ~(::number | manifest | has ::number/add)
    | proves :holdsAChapter
        |~~ The manifest of the first page holds its chapter on values. ~~|
        ~(::qlang | manifest | has ::qlang/values)
    | proves :holdsTheInputsOfAVerb
        |~~ The manifest of a verb holds its inputs. ~~|
        ~(::vec/take | manifest | has ::vec/take/count)
    | proves :holdsTheNamesOfAReader
        |~~ The manifest of a reader's noun holds the names declared under
            it.
        ~~|
        ~(:Box |~~ A box. ~~| | :Box/area ~(1) | ::Box | manifest | eq #[::Box/area])
    | proves :leavesTheLawsToLaws
        |~~ The laws of a name are no part of its manifest. ~~|
        ~(::number/add | get [~(manifest | filter ~(binding | value | is ::law) | count) ~(laws | count | gt 0)] | eq [0 true])
    | proves :readsTheKindOfAValue
        |~~ A value reads the names under the kind it shows. ~~|
        ~(get [5 :number] | map ~manifest | eq (get [~(::number | manifest) ~(::keyword | manifest)]))
    | proves :readsPastATagOfIdentity
        |~~ A value under a tag nothing declares reads the kind beneath it. ~~|
        ~(::Box{:a 1} | manifest | eq (::map | manifest))
    | proves :UnknownNameError/refusesANameNothingDeclares
        |~~ A name nothing declares is refused. ~~|
        ~(::Nothing | manifest
          !| get [~(tag) ~(name)]
          |  eq [::any/manifest/UnknownNameError ::Nothing])
    | proves :readsTheNodeUnderAnAnchor
        |~~ An anchor reads the names under the node the name holds under
            it.
        ~~|
        ~(::vec | manifest :take | eq (::vec/take | manifest))
    | proves :anchor/KindNotTakenError/refusesANounGivenAsTheAnchor
        |~~ A noun given as the anchor is refused; it is the subject. ~~|
        ~(::qlang | manifest ::vec !| tag | eq ::any/manifest/anchor/KindNotTakenError)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:any/laws
  |~~ Answers the laws declared under the name the subject reads, as
      `doc` reads it, in the order of their declarations: a map from
      each law's name, which says the claim it proves, to the pair of its
      page, the sentence of the claim, and its query, which answers
      true. A value reads the laws of its kind, and a name with no laws
      answers `{}`. `runLaws` runs them, and `tutorial` pairs them with
      the page. A name nothing declares is refused,
      `::any/laws/UnknownNameError`. An anchor, a keyword after it, reads
      the laws of the node the name holds under it, as `doc` reads one,
      `::qlang | laws :errors`.
      ::link~(::any/runLaws | doc) ::link~(::any/tutorial | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :anchor
        |~~ The last name of the node whose laws the call answers, a
            keyword, null when the call gives none.
        ~~|
        ~(query /0) #[::keyword ::null]
    | throws :anchor/KindNotTakenError
        |~~ The anchor is a keyword or null, the last name of a node under
            the subject, `::qlang | laws :errors`.
        ~~|
    | returns
        |~~ The name of each law and the pair of its page and its query. ~~|
        ::map
    | throws :UnknownNameError
        |~~ `laws` found no declaration of the name it read, as `binding`
            finds none.
            ::link~(::any/binding/UnknownNameError | doc)
        ~~|
        ::any/UnknownNameError
    | proves :answersEachLawByItsName
        |~~ A law declared under a name is answered under its own name. ~~|
        ~(:k 5 | :k/answersFive |~~ k answers five. ~~| ::law~(k | eq 5)
          | ::k | laws | keys | eq #[:answersFive])
    | proves :pairsThePageWithTheQuery
        |~~ Each law is the pair of its page and its query. ~~|
        ~(:k 5 | :k/answersFive |~~ k answers five. ~~| ::law~(k | eq 5)
          | ::k | laws | get /answersFive | get [~(get /0 | content) /1]
          | eq ["k answers five." ~(k | eq 5)])
    | proves :readsTheLawsOfTheKindOfAValue
        |~~ A value reads the laws of its kind. ~~|
        ~(5 | laws | eq (::number | laws))
    | proves :answersNoLawsAsAnEmptyMap
        |~~ A name with no laws answers an empty map. ~~|
        ~(:k 5 | ::k | laws | eq {})
    | proves :UnknownNameError/refusesANameNothingDeclares
        |~~ A name nothing declares is refused. ~~|
        ~(::nonexistent-binding | laws
          !| get [~(tag) ~(name)]
          |  eq [::any/laws/UnknownNameError ::nonexistent-binding])
    | proves :readsTheLawsUnderAnAnchor
        |~~ An anchor reads the laws of the node the name holds under it.
        ~~|
        ~(::qlang | laws :errors | eq (::qlang/errors | laws))
    | proves :anchor/KindNotTakenError/refusesANumber
        |~~ An anchor that is a number is refused under the input. ~~|
        ~(::qlang | laws 1 !| tag | eq ::any/laws/anchor/KindNotTakenError)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:any/runLaws
  |~~ Answers the results of the laws under the name the subject reads,
      as `doc` reads it, a verb by its address among them, one
      `{:law :says :actual :error :ok}` per law in their order: `:law`
      its address, `:says` its page, the claim it proves, `:actual` what
      its query answered, null where it answered an error, which `:error`
      holds as the error at rest, and `:ok` whether the answer is true,
      so a law that answers false or an error fails. A value runs the
      laws of its kind, and a name of the scope the laws declared under
      it. The anchor, a keyword after it, runs the laws of the node the
      name holds under it, a chapter of the language among them. A name
      nothing declares is refused under the verb,
      `::any/runLaws/UnknownNameError`.
      ::link~(::any/laws | doc) ::link~(::any/tutorial | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :anchor
        |~~ The last name of the node whose laws run, a keyword, null when
            the call gives none.
        ~~|
        ~(query /0) #[::keyword ::null]
    | throws :anchor/KindNotTakenError
        |~~ The anchor is a keyword or null, the last name of a node under
            the subject, `::vec | runLaws :sort`.
        ~~|
    | returns
        |~~ A map for each law: the law, its claim, what it answered and
            whether it held.
        ~~|
        ::vec
    | throws :UnknownNameError
        |~~ `runLaws` found no declaration of the name it read, as
            `binding` finds none.
            ::link~(::any/binding/UnknownNameError | doc)
        ~~|
        ::any/UnknownNameError
    | proves :runsTheLawsOfAVerb
        |~~ The first law of `add` holds. ~~|
        ~(::number/add | runLaws | first | get /ok)
    | proves :holdsEveryLawOfAdd
        |~~ Every law of `add` holds. ~~|
        ~(::number/add | runLaws | map /ok | toSet | eq #[true])
    | proves :failsALawThatAnswersFalse
        |~~ A law that answers false fails. ~~|
        ~(:k 5 | :k/wrong |~~ One is two. ~~| ::law~(1 | eq 2)
          | ::k | runLaws | first | get /ok | not)
    | proves :runsTheLawsOfTheKindOfAValue
        |~~ A value runs the laws of its kind, as `doc` reads its page. ~~|
        ~(5 | runLaws | count | eq (::number | runLaws | count))
    | proves :runsTheLawsUnderTheName
        |~~ The laws under the name a keyword reads run. ~~|
        ~(:limit |~~ A limit. ~~| 10 | :limit/holds |~~ It holds. ~~| ::law~(true)
          | ::limit | runLaws | map /ok | eq [true])
    | proves :runsTheLawsUnderAnAnchor
        |~~ An anchor runs the laws of the name under the one read. ~~|
        ~(::qlang | runLaws :values | every /ok)
    | proves :anchor/KindNotTakenError/refusesANumber
        |~~ An anchor that is a number is refused under the input. ~~|
        ~(::qlang | runLaws 1 !| tag | eq ::any/runLaws/anchor/KindNotTakenError)
    | proves :UnknownNameError/refusesANameNothingDeclares
        |~~ A name nothing declares is refused. ~~|
        ~(::nonexistent-binding | runLaws
          !| get [~(tag) ~(name)]
          |  eq [::any/runLaws/UnknownNameError ::nonexistent-binding])
    | proves :UnknownNameError/refusesATagNothingDeclares
        |~~ A tag nothing declares is refused. ~~|
        ~(::Nonexistent | runLaws
          !| get [~(tag) ~(name)]
          |  eq [::any/runLaws/UnknownNameError ::Nonexistent])
    | proves :UnknownNameError/namesWhereItsVerbsLive
        |~~ The name of a verb is refused with the addresses where the
            verbs of that name live.
        ~~|
        ~(::filter | runLaws !| addresses | has ::vec/filter)
    | proves :failsALawThatAnswersAFailure
        |~~ A law that answers an error fails, and `:error` holds it at
            rest.
        ~~|
        ~(:k 5 | :k/bad |~~ b ~~| ::law~(add "x") | ::k | runLaws | first
          | get [/ok /actual ~(get /error | tag)] | eq [false null ::number/add/SubjectNotNumberError])
    | impl ::builtin{}

:any/tutorial
  |~~ Answers the page of the name the subject reads and its laws, the
      pair a reader learns the name from: the prose that says what it
      is, and the laws that show it at work, each the pair of its claim
      and its query, `::number | tutorial`. A value reads the page and
      the laws of its kind. A name nothing declares is refused as `doc`
      refuses it, under the tag of `doc`. The call takes no anchor: a
      node is read by its address, `::vec/take | tutorial`.
      ::link~(::any/doc | doc) ::link~(::any/laws | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ The page and the laws. ~~| ::vec
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `tutorial` reads the name of its subject and takes no anchor:
            a node is read by its address, `::vec/take | tutorial`.
        ~~|
    | proves :pairsThePageWithTheLaws
        |~~ A tutorial is the page of a name and its laws. ~~|
        ~(::number | tutorial | get [~(get /0 | tag) ~(get /1 | tag)] | eq [::doc ::map])
    | proves :readsTheKindOfAValue
        |~~ A value reads the page and the laws of its kind. ~~|
        ~(5 | tutorial | eq (::number | tutorial))
    | proves :refusesANameNothingDeclares
        |~~ A name nothing declares is refused as `doc` refuses it. ~~|
        ~(::nothere | tutorial !| tag | eq ::any/doc/UnknownNameError)
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesAnAnchor
        |~~ An anchor given to `tutorial` is refused under the verb. ~~|
        ~(::vec | tutorial :take !| tag | eq ::any/tutorial/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | impl ~(:subject it | doc | get [~(it) ~(subject | laws)])

:any/tag
  |~~ Answers the tag of the subject where the call gives no name, one side
      of the value, whose other side `payload` answers, and lays the tag the
      call names over the subject where it gives one. Given no name it
      answers the kind of the subject, its outermost tag: `5 | tag`
      answers `::number` and `::Box::Crate{:a 1} | tag` `::Box`; a literal
      its kind, `"hi"`, `:foo`, `[1 2]` and `#[1 2]` answering `::string`,
      `::keyword`, `::vec` and `::set`; null `::null`; a quote, a doc and a
      tag name `::quote`, `::doc` and `::tag`; and an error at rest the tag
      of the refusal it carries, while `is` walks the tags to the kind
      beneath. Given a tag name it lays the tag over the subject through
      its kind as the literal does where it is written: `5 | tag ::Box`
      answers `::Box#5`, its tag the name and its payload the subject, so
      a value splits and joins back into itself,
      `:t (tag) | payload | tag t`. A tag the subject is already of, as
      `is` answers, changes nothing, `5 | tag ::number`, `5 | tag ::any`
      and `#[1] | tag ::vec` answering the subject and
      `:Lab |~~ A lab. ~~| | ::Lab::Lab[1]` answering `::Lab[1]`, a subject
      that holds it at its head held to its kind again; any other stands
      before the tags of the subject and never instead of them, and
      replacing the tag goes through the payload, `payload | tag ::Crate`.
      A tag is laid through its kind, which takes the payload its `accepts`
      names by the payload's own tag, so a payload of another kind or
      under another tag is refused, `"5" | tag ::number`, a kind the reader
      declares holds its invariants, and a kind whose `accepts` names no kind
      takes none, `5 | tag ::tagged`. A tag over an
      error at rest names its site, `!{:by 3} | tag ::Late` answering
      `::Late!{:by 3}`, and `raise` raises it under that tag. The call
      takes one name; a keyword, `:box`, names a field and no kind.
      ::link~(::any/payload | doc) ::link~(::any/type | doc) ::link~(::any/is | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :name
        |~~ The tag laid over the subject, null when the call gives none
            and the tag of the subject is read.
        ~~|
        ~(query /0) #[::null ::tag]
    | throws :name/KindNotTakenError
        |~~ The name is a tag name, `::Box`; a keyword, `:box`, names a
            field and no kind.
        ~~|
    | returns
        |~~ The tag of the subject, or the subject under the tag, read by
            its kind.
        ~~|
        ::any
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `tag` lays one tag: two tags nest one call after the other,
            `tag ::Box | tag ::Crate`.
        ~~|
    | proves :readsTheKindOfEveryValue
        |~~ Given no name, `tag` answers the kind of a literal, of null, of
            a quote, of a doc and of a tag name.
        ~~|
        ~([42 "hi" :foo [1 2] #[1 2] null ~(42) |~~ A page. ~~| ::vec] | map ~tag
          | eq [::number ::string ::keyword ::vec ::set ::null ::quote ::doc ::tag])
    | proves :readsTheOutermostTag
        |~~ A value under tags answers the outermost. ~~|
        ~(::Box::Crate{:a 1} | tag | eq ::Box)
    | proves :readsAnErrorAtRest
        |~~ The error at rest answers the tag of its refusal. ~~|
        ~("x" | add 1 !| tag | eq ::number/add/SubjectNotNumberError)
    | proves :standsOverTheValue
        |~~ The payload of a tagged number is the number. ~~|
        ~(42 | tag ::Box | payload | eq 42)
    | proves :namesTheKind
        |~~ The tag laid is the tag read. ~~|
        ~([1 2 3] | tag ::Triple | tag | eq ::Triple)
    | proves :splitsAndJoinsEveryValue
        |~~ A value split into its tag and its payload joins back into
            itself, whatever its kind.
        ~~|
        ~([42 "hi" :foo true null /a #"a" ::Box [1 2] {:a 1} #[2 1] ~(add 1) |~~ A page. ~~| ::Box#5 ::A::B{} !{:k 1}]
          | map ~(:value it | :t (tag) | payload | tag t | eq value) | every ~(it))
    | proves :laysThroughTheKind
        |~~ A tag of a kind of the core reads the payload as the kind. ~~|
        ~(5 | tag ::number | eq 5)
    | proves :refusesAPayloadTheKindRefuses
        |~~ A payload the kind refuses is refused by the kind. ~~|
        ~("5" | tag ::number !| tag | eq ::number/PayloadKindNotTakenError)
    | proves :holdsTheInvariantsOfAReadersKind
        |~~ A kind the reader declares holds its invariants. ~~|
        ~(:Pos |~~ A positive number. ~~| (::kind | holds :NotPositiveError |~~ A number above zero. ~~| ~(gt 0)) | -3 | tag ::Pos !| tag | eq ::Pos/NotPositiveError)
    | proves :laysNoTagTheSubjectIsOf
        |~~ A tag the subject is already of, as `is` answers, changes
            nothing, and one at its head stands once.
        ~~|
        ~(:Lab |~~ A lab. ~~| | get [~(5 | tag ::number) ~(#[1] | tag ::vec) ~(::Box#5 | tag ::number) ~(::Lab::Lab[1] | payload)]
          | eq [5 #[1] ::Box#5 [1]])
    | proves :holdsTheHeadToItsKindAgain
        |~~ A subject that holds the tag at its head is held to its kind
            again, a value laid before its kind was declared among them.
        ~~|
        ~(::Pos#-3 | :Pos |~~ A positive number. ~~| (::kind | holds :NotPositiveError |~~ Above zero. ~~| ~(gt 0))
          | tag ::Pos
          !| tag
          |  eq ::Pos/NotPositiveError)
    | proves :refusesAPayloadUnderAnotherTag
        |~~ A kind takes its payload by the payload's own tag. ~~|
        ~(:Nums |~~ Numbers. ~~| (::kind | accepts |~~ The numbers. ~~| ::vec) | ::T[1 2] | tag ::Nums
          !| get [~(tag) ~(actualTag)]
          |  eq [::Nums/PayloadKindNotTakenError ::T])
    | proves :nestsATaggedValue
        |~~ A tag over a tagged value nests it. ~~|
        ~(:Inner |~~ An inner kind. ~~| | :Outer |~~ An outer kind. ~~|
          | ::Inner[1] | tag ::Outer | payload | tag | eq ::Inner)
    | proves :putsAnotherTagOverThePayload
        |~~ Another tag goes over the payload taken out. ~~|
        ~(::Box#1 | payload | tag ::Crate | eq ::Crate#1)
    | proves :namesTheSiteOfAnErrorAtRest
        |~~ A tag over an error at rest names its site. ~~|
        ~(!{:by 3} | tag ::Late | eq ::Late!{:by 3})
    | proves :raisesAnErrorAtRestAsItself
        |~~ An error at rest split and joined back raises as itself. ~~|
        ~(1 | add "1" !| :t (tag) | payload | tag t | raise
          !| tag
          |  eq ::number/add/addend/KindNotTakenError)
    | proves :name/KindNotTakenError/refusesAKeyword
        |~~ A keyword given as the name is refused under the input. ~~|
        ~(5 | tag :box !| get [~(tag) ~(actualTag)] | eq [::any/tag/name/KindNotTakenError ::keyword])
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesTwoTags
        |~~ Two tags given to one call are refused under the verb. ~~|
        ~(5 | tag ::Box ::Crate !| tag | eq ::any/tag/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:any/payload
  |~~ Answers the value beneath the outermost tag of the subject, the other
      side of `tag`, so a value splits and joins back into itself,
      `:t (tag) | payload | tag t`: `::Box[42] | payload` answers `[42]`,
      and a value under two tags opens one, `::A::B[1] | payload`
      answering `::B[1]`. A value of a kind of the core answers what its
      literal writes after the tag of its kind: a number, a string, a
      vector and a map are their own, `1 | payload` answering `1`, and a
      set, a quote and a doc answer the vector of their elements, of their
      steps and of their segments, `#[2 1] | payload` answering `[1 2]`. A
      value turned by `desc` opens to the value it turned, and an error at
      rest to its facts. `within` edits the payload and puts the tag back.
      ::link~(::any/tag | doc) ::link~(::tagged/within | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ The value beneath the outermost tag, of any kind. ~~| ::any
    | proves :answersAVector
        |~~ A box of a vector answers the vector. ~~|
        ~(:Box |~~ A box. ~~| | ::Box[42] | payload | eq [42])
    | proves :answersAString
        |~~ A note of a string answers the string. ~~|
        ~(:Note |~~ A note. ~~| | ::Note"hello" | payload | eq "hello")
    | proves :undoesTag
        |~~ A value laid under a tag is its payload. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | tag ::Foo | payload | eq {:a 1})
    | proves :opensOneTagOfTwo
        |~~ A value under two tags opens one. ~~|
        ~(::A::B[1] | payload | eq ::B[1])
    | proves :answersTheVectorOfASet
        |~~ A set's payload is its vector in its one order. ~~|
        ~(#[2 1] | payload | eq [1 2])
    | proves :answersAValueOfItsOwn
        |~~ A number, a string, a vector and a map are their own payload.
        ~~|
        ~([1 "s" [1] {:a 1}] | map ~payload | eq [1 "s" [1] {:a 1}])
    | proves :opensAnErrorAtRestToItsFacts
        |~~ An error at rest opens to its facts. ~~|
        ~(!{:k 1} | payload | eq {:k 1})
    | impl ::builtin{}

:any/sumOf
  |~~ Answers the sum of the numbers the call computes against the
      subject, as many as it gives, the subject only what they are read
      from: two fields of one record add by `sumOf /x /y`,
      `{:x 10 :y 3} | sumOf /x /y` answering `13`. A field the record
      lacks answers the refusal of its read, `::path/KeyNotInMapError`,
      and one that may be missing is read with its default,
      `(query /y | coalesce 0)`. Text that holds a
      number is read first by `toNumber`, and the numbers of a vector
      sum by `sum`. The call gives one number at least.
      ::link~(::number/add | doc) ::link~(::vec/sum | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :numbers
        |~~ The numbers to add, one at least, each a number or a path the
            verb reads from the subject.
        ~~|
        ~(it) [#[::number ::path]]
    | holds :numbers/EmptyError
        |~~ `sumOf` adds the numbers the call gives, one at least,
            `sumOf /x /y`.
        ~~|
        ~(count | gt 0)
    | throws :numbers/ElementKindNotTakenError
        |~~ Each of the numbers is a number or a path; text that holds one
            is read by `toNumber`, `sumOf (get /x | toNumber) /y`.
        ~~|
    | returns |~~ The sum of the numbers, under the tags they share. ~~| ::number
    | proves :addsTwoFields
        |~~ Two fields of one map add. ~~|
        ~({:x 10 :y 3} | sumOf /x /y | eq 13)
    | proves :addsAsManyAsGiven
        |~~ As many fields as the call gives add. ~~|
        ~({:a 1 :b 2 :c 3} | sumOf /a /b /c | eq 6)
    | proves :answersTheErrorOfAMissingField
        |~~ A field the record lacks answers the refusal of its read. ~~|
        ~({:x 1} | sumOf /x /y !| tag | eq ::path/KeyNotInMapError)
    | proves :readsAMissingFieldByItsDefault
        |~~ A field that may be missing is read with its default. ~~|
        ~({:x 1} | sumOf /x (query /y | coalesce 0) | eq 1)
    | proves :readsTextByToNumber
        |~~ Text that holds a number is read first by `toNumber`. ~~|
        ~({:x "1" :y 2} | sumOf (get /x | toNumber) /y | eq 3)
    | proves :numbers/EmptyError/refusesNoNumber
        |~~ A call with no number is refused under the input. ~~|
        ~({} | sumOf !| tag | eq ::any/sumOf/numbers/EmptyError)
    | proves :numbers/ElementKindNotTakenError/refusesText
        |~~ Text is no number, and is refused at its index. ~~|
        ~({} | sumOf "1"
          !| get [~(tag) ~(index)]
          |  eq [::any/sumOf/numbers/ElementKindNotTakenError 0])
    | impl ~(get numbers | sum)

:any/mulOf
  |~~ Answers the product of the factors the call computes against the
      subject, as many as it gives: the price and the quantity of one line
      multiply by `mulOf /price /qty`,
      `{:price 4 :qty 3} | mulOf /price /qty` answering `12`. One factor
      answers itself, and the call gives one at least. Text that holds a
      number is read first by `toNumber`.
      ::link~(::number/mul | doc) ::link~(::any/sumOf | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :factors
        |~~ The numbers multiplied, one at least, each a number or a path
            the verb reads from the subject.
        ~~|
        ~(it) [#[::number ::path]]
    | holds :factors/EmptyError
        |~~ `mulOf` multiplies the factors the call gives, one at least,
            `mulOf /price /qty`.
        ~~|
        ~(count | gt 0)
    | throws :factors/ElementKindNotTakenError
        |~~ Each of the factors is a number or a path; text that holds one
            is read by `toNumber`.
        ~~|
    | returns |~~ The factors multiplied, under the tags they share. ~~| ::number
    | proves :multipliesAsManyAsGiven
        |~~ As many factors as the call gives multiply. ~~|
        ~({:a 2 :b 3 :c 4} | mulOf /a /b /c | eq 24)
    | proves :answersOneFactor
        |~~ One factor answers itself. ~~|
        ~({:a 7} | mulOf /a | eq 7)
    | proves :factors/EmptyError/refusesNoFactor
        |~~ A call with no factor is refused under the input. ~~|
        ~({:a 2} | mulOf !| tag | eq ::any/mulOf/factors/EmptyError)
    | proves :factors/ElementKindNotTakenError/refusesAString
        |~~ A string is no factor, and is refused at its index. ~~|
        ~({:a 2} | mulOf /a "x"
          !| get [~(tag) ~(index)]
          |  eq [::any/mulOf/factors/ElementKindNotTakenError 1])
    | proves :multipliesTwoFields
        |~~ The price and the quantity of one line multiply. ~~|
        ~({:price 4 :qty 3} | mulOf /price /qty | eq 12)
    | impl ~(get factors | reduce 1 ~(mul))

:any/subOf
  |~~ Answers the minuend the call computes against the subject less the
      subtrahend: what is left to pay is `subOf /total /paid`,
      `{:total 10 :paid 3} | subOf /total /paid` answering `7`. The call
      gives the two, the minuend first; a sum subtracted is computed as
      the subtrahend, `subOf /total (sumOf /a /b)`.
      ::link~(::number/sub | doc) ::link~(::any/sumOf | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :minuend
        |~~ The number subtracted from. ~~|
        /0 #[::number ::path]
    | throws :minuend/KindNotTakenError
        |~~ The minuend is a number or a path,
            `{:a 5 :b 3} | subOf /a /b`; text that holds one is read by
            `toNumber` first, `subOf (get /a | toNumber) /b`.
        ~~|
    | takes :subtrahend |~~ The number subtracted. ~~| /1 #[::number ::path]
    | throws :subtrahend/KindNotTakenError
        |~~ The subtrahend is a number or a path,
            `{:a 5 :b 3} | subOf /a /b`; text that holds one is read by
            `toNumber` first, `subOf /a (get /b | toNumber)`.
        ~~|
    | throws :subtrahend/NotGivenError
        |~~ `subOf` subtracts the subtrahend from the minuend and was
            given the minuend alone: `subOf /total /paid`.
        ~~|
    | returns |~~ The minuend less the subtrahend, under the tags they share. ~~| ::number
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `subOf` subtracts one number from another: a sum subtracted is
            computed as the subtrahend, `subOf /total (sumOf /a /b)`.
        ~~|
    | proves :subtractsASumComputedAsTheSubtrahend
        |~~ A sum subtracted is computed as the subtrahend. ~~|
        ~({:total 10 :a 3 :b 2} | subOf /total (sumOf /a /b) | eq 5)
    | proves :minuend/KindNotTakenError/refusesAString
        |~~ A minuend that is a string is refused under the input. ~~|
        ~({:p 1} | subOf "a" /p
          !| get [~(tag) ~(actualTag)]
          |  eq [::any/subOf/minuend/KindNotTakenError ::string])
    | proves :subtrahend/NotGivenError/refusesOneNumber
        |~~ A call with the minuend alone is refused under the subtrahend.
        ~~|
        ~({:t 10} | subOf /t !| tag | eq ::any/subOf/subtrahend/NotGivenError)
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesAThirdNumber
        |~~ A third number is refused under the verb. ~~|
        ~({:t 10 :p 3 :q 1} | subOf /t /p /q !| tag | eq ::any/subOf/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | proves :subtrahend/KindNotTakenError/refusesAString
        |~~ A subtrahend that is no number is refused under the input. ~~|
        ~({:a 5} | subOf /a "3" !| tag | eq ::any/subOf/subtrahend/KindNotTakenError)
    | proves :subtractsTwoFields
        |~~ What is left to pay is the total less what was paid. ~~|
        ~({:total 10 :paid 3} | subOf /total /paid | eq 7)
    | impl
        ~(get (get [~(minuend) ~(subtrahend)])
          | :pair it
          | (pair | first)
          | sub (pair | last))

:any/divOf
  |~~ Answers the dividend the call computes against the subject divided
      by the divisor: a mean is `divOf /sum /count`,
      `{:sum 20 :count 4} | divOf /sum /count` answering `5`. A quotient
      that falls between whole numbers is a decimal, and a divisor of
      zero is refused as `div` refuses it. The call gives the two, the
      dividend first; a product to divide by is computed as the divisor,
      `divOf /total (mulOf /a /b)`.
      ::link~(::number/div | doc) ::link~(::any/mulOf | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :dividend |~~ The number divided. ~~| /0 #[::number ::path]
    | throws :dividend/KindNotTakenError
        |~~ The dividend is a number or a path,
            `{:a 6 :b 3} | divOf /a /b`; text that holds one is read by
            `toNumber` first, `divOf (get /a | toNumber) /b`.
        ~~|
    | takes :divisor |~~ The number divided by. ~~| /1 #[::number ::path]
    | throws :divisor/KindNotTakenError
        |~~ The divisor is a number or a path,
            `{:a 6 :b 3} | divOf /a /b`; text that holds one is read by
            `toNumber` first, `divOf /a (get /b | toNumber)`.
        ~~|
    | throws :divisor/NotGivenError
        |~~ `divOf` divides the dividend by the divisor and was given the
            dividend alone: `divOf /sum /count`.
        ~~|
    | returns |~~ The dividend divided by the divisor, under the tags they share. ~~| ::number
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `divOf` divides one number by another: a product to divide by is
            computed as the divisor, `divOf /total (mulOf /a /b)`.
        ~~|
    | proves :dividesByTheSecond
        |~~ A mean is the sum over the count. ~~|
        ~({:sum 20 :count 4} | divOf /sum /count | eq 5)
    | proves :answersADecimal
        |~~ A quotient between whole numbers is a decimal. ~~|
        ~({:sum 7 :count 2} | divOf /sum /count | eq 3.5)
    | proves :refusesADivisorOfZero
        |~~ A divisor of zero is refused as `div` refuses it. ~~|
        ~({:sum 20 :count 0} | divOf /sum /count !| tag | eq ::number/div/divisor/ZeroError)
    | proves :divisor/NotGivenError/refusesOneNumber
        |~~ A call with the dividend alone is refused under the divisor. ~~|
        ~({:s 20} | divOf /s !| tag | eq ::any/divOf/divisor/NotGivenError)
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesAThirdNumber
        |~~ A third number is refused under the verb. ~~|
        ~({:s 20 :c 4 :x 1} | divOf /s /c /x !| tag | eq ::any/divOf/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | proves :dividend/KindNotTakenError/refusesAString
        |~~ A dividend that is no number is refused under the input. ~~|
        ~({:b 3} | divOf "6" /b !| tag | eq ::any/divOf/dividend/KindNotTakenError)
    | proves :divisor/KindNotTakenError/refusesAString
        |~~ A divisor that is no number is refused under the input. ~~|
        ~({:a 6} | divOf /a "3" !| tag | eq ::any/divOf/divisor/KindNotTakenError)
    | impl
        ~(get (get [~(dividend) ~(divisor)])
          | :pair it
          | (pair | first)
          | div (pair | last))

:any/allOf
  |~~ Answers whether every condition the call gives holds of the subject:
      two conditions of one record hold together by
      `allOf (get /active) (get /age | gte 18)`. Each modifier is a
      condition computed at the call, every one of them, or code that
      answers one in its turn, a quote or a path, run on the subject once every condition
      before it held: a condition that fails where an earlier one does not
      hold is written as such a quote,
      `allOf (get /email | ne null) ~(get /email | endsWith ".org")`, and
      the first condition that does not hold answers `false` with the
      quotes after it unrun; a quote that fails answers its error. The
      call gives one condition at least.
      ::link~(::any/anyOf | doc) ::link~(::vec/every | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :conditions
        |~~ The conditions, booleans and quotes that answer them, one at
            least.
        ~~|
        ~(it) [#[::boolean ::path ::quote]]
    | holds :conditions/EmptyError
        |~~ `allOf` asks whether every condition the call gives holds, one
            at least, `allOf (get /active) (get /age | gte 18)`.
        ~~|
        ~(count | gt 0)
    | throws :conditions/ElementKindNotTakenError
        |~~ Each modifier of `allOf` is a condition computed at the call,
            `(get /age | gte 18)`, a path whose field answers one,
            `/active`, or a quote that answers one in its turn,
            `~(get /email | endsWith ".org")`. A condition computed at the
            call is computed at every index, the first among them, and one
            that answered an error is refused here, the error its value: a
            field a record may lack is read softly,
            `allOf (query /b | eq 1)`, and a condition that fails where an
            earlier one does not hold is written as a quote that answers
            it in its turn, which runs only once every condition before it
            held.
        ~~|
    | returns |~~ Whether every condition holds. ~~| ::boolean
    | throws :ConditionKindNotTakenError
        |~~ A quote or a path among the conditions of `allOf` answers a
            boolean when it runs; `:index` names, from zero, the condition
            whose code answered another kind, and a quote that reads a
            field compares it to answer a condition,
            `~(get /email | ne null)`.
        ~~|
        ::fn/ElementKindNotTakenError
    | proves :holdsWhenEveryConditionHolds
        |~~ An active adult passes both conditions. ~~|
        ~({:active true :age 25} | allOf (get /active) (get /age | gte 18))
    | proves :failsWhenOneFails
        |~~ A minor fails the second. ~~|
        ~({:active true :age 15} | allOf (get /active) (get /age | gte 18) | not)
    | proves :runsAQuoteInItsTurn
        |~~ A quote runs once the conditions before it held. ~~|
        ~([{:email null} {:email "a@b.org"}]
          | map ~(allOf (get /email | ne null) ~(get /email | endsWith ".org"))
          | eq [false true])
    | proves :answersTheFailureOfAQuote
        |~~ A quote that fails answers its error. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | allOf ~(get /b | eq 1) !| get [~(tag) ~(key)] | eq [::path/KeyNotInMapError :b])
    | proves :conditions/ElementKindNotTakenError/refusesAFieldMissingAtTheFirstIndex
        |~~ A field the record lacks is refused at its index, the first
            among them.
        ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | allOf (get /b | eq 1)
          !| get [~(tag) ~(index) ~(actualTag)]
          |  eq [::any/allOf/conditions/ElementKindNotTakenError 0 ::path/KeyNotInMapError])
    | proves :conditions/ElementKindNotTakenError/readsAMissingFieldSoftly
        |~~ A field the record may lack is read softly. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | allOf (query /b | eq 1) | not)
    | proves :conditions/ElementKindNotTakenError/refusesAConditionThatFailedAtTheCall
        |~~ A condition computed at the call is computed even where an
            earlier one does not hold, and its error is refused at its
            index.
        ~~|
        ~({:age 15} | allOf (get /age | gte 18) (get /licence)
          !| get [~(tag) ~(index) ~(actualTag)]
          |  eq [::any/allOf/conditions/ElementKindNotTakenError 1 ::path/KeyNotInMapError])
    | proves :conditions/ElementKindNotTakenError/refusesANumber
        |~~ A number is no condition, and is refused at its index. ~~|
        ~({:age 15} | allOf (get /age)
          !| get [~(tag) ~(index) ~(actualTag)]
          |  eq [::any/allOf/conditions/ElementKindNotTakenError 0 ::number])
    | proves :ConditionKindNotTakenError/refusesAQuoteThatAnswersAString
        |~~ A quote that answers a string is refused at its index. ~~|
        ~({:email "a@b.org"} | allOf true /email
          !| get [~(tag) ~(index) ~(actualTag) ~(expected)]
          |  eq [::any/allOf/ConditionKindNotTakenError 1 ::string #[::boolean]])
    | proves :ConditionKindNotTakenError/holdsAQuoteThatCompares
        |~~ A quote that compares the field it reads answers a condition.
        ~~|
        ~({:email "a@b.org"} | allOf true ~(get /email | ne null))
    | proves :conditions/EmptyError/refusesNoCondition
        |~~ A call with no condition is refused under the input. ~~|
        ~({} | allOf !| tag | eq ::any/allOf/conditions/EmptyError)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:any/anyOf
  |~~ Answers whether some condition the call gives holds of the subject:
      a high score passes without the flag,
      `anyOf /vip (get /score | gt 95)`. Each modifier is a condition
      computed at the call or code that answers one in its turn, a quote
      or a path, run on
      the subject once no condition before it held, as for `allOf`: the
      first that holds answers `true` with the quotes after it unrun, so a
      flagged record passes without the field its quote reads,
      `anyOf /vip ~(get /score | gt 95)`, and a quote that fails answers
      its error. The call gives one condition at least.
      ::link~(::any/allOf | doc) ::link~(::vec/any | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :conditions
        |~~ The conditions, booleans and quotes that answer them, one at
            least.
        ~~|
        ~(it) [#[::boolean ::path ::quote]]
    | holds :conditions/EmptyError
        |~~ `anyOf` asks whether some condition the call gives holds, one
            at least, `anyOf (get /vip) (get /score | gt 95)`.
        ~~|
        ~(count | gt 0)
    | throws :conditions/ElementKindNotTakenError
        |~~ Each modifier of `anyOf` is a condition computed at the call,
            `(get /score | gt 95)`, a path whose field answers one,
            `/vip`, or a quote that answers one in its turn,
            `~(get /score | gt 95)`. A condition computed at the call is
            computed at every index, the first among them, and one that
            answered an error is refused here, the error its value: a
            field a record may lack is read softly,
            `anyOf (query /vip | eq true)`, and a condition that fails
            where an earlier one holds is written as a quote that answers
            it in its turn, which runs only once no condition before it
            held.
        ~~|
    | returns |~~ Whether some condition holds. ~~| ::boolean
    | throws :ConditionKindNotTakenError
        |~~ A quote or a path among the conditions of `anyOf` answers a
            boolean when it runs; `:index` names, from zero, the condition
            whose code answered another kind, and a quote that reads a
            field compares it to answer a condition,
            `~(get /score | gt 95)`.
        ~~|
        ::fn/ElementKindNotTakenError
    | proves :holdsWhenOneHolds
        |~~ A high score passes without the flag. ~~|
        ~({:vip false :score 99} | anyOf (get /vip) (get /score | gt 95))
    | proves :failsWhenNoneHolds
        |~~ A low score without the flag fails. ~~|
        ~({:vip false :score 50} | anyOf (get /vip) (get /score | gt 95) | not)
    | proves :runsAQuoteInItsTurn
        |~~ A flagged record passes without the field the quote reads. ~~|
        ~([{:vip true} {:vip false :score 99}]
          | map ~(anyOf (get /vip) ~(get /score | gt 95))
          | eq [true true])
    | proves :answersTheFailureOfAQuote
        |~~ A quote that fails answers its error. ~~|
        ~({:vip false} | anyOf (get /vip) ~(get /score | gt 95) !| get [~(tag) ~(key)] | eq [::path/KeyNotInMapError :score])
    | proves :conditions/ElementKindNotTakenError/refusesAFieldMissingAtTheFirstIndex
        |~~ A field the record lacks is refused at its index, the first
            among them.
        ~~|
        ~({:score 99} | anyOf (get /vip | eq true)
          !| get [~(tag) ~(index) ~(actualTag)]
          |  eq [::any/anyOf/conditions/ElementKindNotTakenError 0 ::path/KeyNotInMapError])
    | proves :conditions/ElementKindNotTakenError/readsAMissingFieldSoftly
        |~~ A field the record may lack is read softly. ~~|
        ~({:score 99} | anyOf (query /vip | eq true) (get /score | gt 95))
    | proves :conditions/ElementKindNotTakenError/refusesAConditionThatFailedAtTheCall
        |~~ A condition computed at the call is computed even where an
            earlier one holds, and its error is refused at its index.
        ~~|
        ~({:vip true} | anyOf (get /vip) (get /score | gt 95)
          !| get [~(tag) ~(index) ~(actualTag)]
          |  eq [::any/anyOf/conditions/ElementKindNotTakenError 1 ::path/KeyNotInMapError])
    | proves :conditions/ElementKindNotTakenError/refusesANumber
        |~~ A number is no condition, and is refused at its index. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | anyOf (get /a)
          !| get [~(tag) ~(index) ~(actualTag)]
          |  eq [::any/anyOf/conditions/ElementKindNotTakenError 0 ::number])
    | proves :ConditionKindNotTakenError/refusesAQuoteThatAnswersANumber
        |~~ A quote that answers a number is refused at its index. ~~|
        ~({:score 99} | anyOf false /score
          !| get [~(tag) ~(index) ~(actualTag) ~(expected)]
          |  eq [::any/anyOf/ConditionKindNotTakenError 1 ::number #[::boolean]])
    | proves :ConditionKindNotTakenError/holdsAQuoteThatCompares
        |~~ A quote that compares the field it reads answers a condition.
        ~~|
        ~({:score 99} | anyOf false ~(get /score | gt 95))
    | proves :conditions/EmptyError/refusesNoCondition
        |~~ A call with no condition is refused under the input. ~~|
        ~({} | anyOf !| tag | eq ::any/anyOf/conditions/EmptyError)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:any/unionOf
  |~~ Answers the union of the parts the call computes against the
      subject, maps or sets, in their order, a later key holding over an
      earlier: the value of a pair with its name beside it is
      `unionOf /1 (get {:name ~(get /0 | toString)})`. Maps unite with maps and sets
      with sets, a map with a set being refused by `union`, and the
      union stands under the tags the parts share. One part answers itself,
      and the call gives one at least.
      ::link~(::map/union | doc) ::link~(::set/union | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :parts |~~ The maps or the sets, one at least. ~~| ~(it)
    | holds :parts/EmptyError
        |~~ `unionOf` unites the parts the call gives, one at least,
            `unionOf /a /b`.
        ~~|
        ~(count | gt 0)
    | returns |~~ The parts united, under the tags they share. ~~| ::any
    | proves :mergesTwoFields
        |~~ Two maps one value holds merge. ~~|
        ~({:a {:x 1} :b {:y 2}} | unionOf /a /b | eq {:x 1 :y 2})
    | proves :holdsTheLaterKey
        |~~ A later key holds over an earlier. ~~|
        ~({:a {:x 1} :b {:x 2}} | unionOf /a /b | eq {:x 2})
    | proves :foldsTheKeyIntoTheValue
        |~~ The key of a pair joins its value as a field. ~~|
        ~([:auth {:port 3000}] | unionOf /1 (get {:name ~(get /0 | toString)}) | eq {:port 3000 :name "auth"})
    | proves :unitesSets
        |~~ Two sets unite. ~~|
        ~({:a #[1] :b #[2]} | unionOf /a /b | eq #[1 2])
    | proves :refusesAMapWithASet
        |~~ A map with a set is refused by `union`. ~~|
        ~({:a {:x 1} :b #[2]} | unionOf /a /b !| tag | eq ::map/union/other/KindNotTakenError)
    | proves :answersUnderTheTagsThePartsShare
        |~~ The union stands under the tags the parts share. ~~|
        ~({:a {:x 1} :b ::Box{:y 2}} | unionOf /a /b | eq ::Box{:x 1 :y 2})
    | proves :answersOnePart
        |~~ One part answers itself. ~~|
        ~({:a {:x 1}} | unionOf /a | eq {:x 1})
    | proves :parts/EmptyError/refusesNoPart
        |~~ A call with no part to unite is refused under `parts`. ~~|
        ~({} | unionOf !| tag | eq ::any/unionOf/parts/EmptyError)
    | impl ~(get parts | :read it | drop 1 | reduce (read | first) ~(union))

:any/is
  |~~ Answers whether the walk of the subject's tags reaches a value of
      one of the kinds the call gives, `5 | is ::number`. A number under
      a tag of its own is a number, while `tag` answers the tag over
      it; a map under a tag is a map; and a set is a vector, as a quote
      and a doc are, while a vector is no set. A failure is asked once
      `!|` catches it, an error at rest, a value of its refusal, whose kind
      closes it, or of `::error` where no refusal declares it, and a value
      of a kind that wires its parts is of that kind alone, `payload`
      answering what lies beneath its tag. Given
      several kinds, it answers whether the subject is of one of them,
      and a call of none is refused. A kind no tag of the walk names is not
      reached, so a misspelt kind, `is ::Numbr`, answers false and is
      not refused. An input of a verb is held to its kinds by the `takes`
      that declares it.
      ::link~(::any/tag | doc) ::link~(::fn/takes | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :kinds
        |~~ The kinds asked, each a tag name, one at least. ~~|
        ~(it) [::tag]
    | throws :kinds/ElementKindNotTakenError
        |~~ Each of the kinds is a tag name, `::number`; a keyword,
            `:number`, names a field and no kind.
        ~~|
    | holds :kinds/EmptyError
        |~~ `is` asks of one kind at least, `5 | is ::number`; a call of
            none asks nothing.
        ~~|
        ~(count | gt 0)
    | returns
        |~~ `true` when the walk of the tags reaches one of the kinds,
            `false` otherwise.
        ~~|
        ::boolean
    | proves :holdsOfItsKind
        |~~ A number is a number. ~~|
        ~(5 | is ::number)
    | proves :walksUnderATag
        |~~ A number under a tag of its own is a number. ~~|
        ~(::Width#5 | is ::number)
    | proves :walksUnderATagToAMap
        |~~ A map under a tag is a map. ~~|
        ~(::Box{:a 1} | is ::map)
    | proves :takesASetForAVector
        |~~ A set is a vector. ~~|
        ~(#[1 2] | is ::vec)
    | proves :takesAQuoteAndADocForVectors
        |~~ A quote is a vector of its steps, and a doc of its segments. ~~|
        ~([~(add 1) |~~ A page. ~~|] | map ~(is ::vec) | eq [true true])
    | proves :keepsAVectorApartFromASet
        |~~ A vector is no set. ~~|
        ~([1] | is ::set | not)
    | proves :asksAnErrorAtRest
        |~~ An error at rest of a refusal is a value of its refusal, which
            closes it, and its payload of `::error`.
        ~~|
        ~("x" | add 1
          !| get [~(is ::number/add/SubjectNotNumberError) ~(is ::error) ~(payload | is ::error)]
          |  eq [true false true])
    | proves :holdsOfOneOfSeveralKinds
        |~~ Of several kinds, the subject is one of them. ~~|
        ~(5 | is ::string ::number)
    | proves :failsOfOtherKinds
        |~~ A string is neither a number nor null. ~~|
        ~("5" | is ::number ::null | not)
    | proves :kinds/EmptyError/refusesACallOfNoKind
        |~~ A call of no kind is refused under the kinds. ~~|
        ~(5 | is !| tag | eq ::any/is/kinds/EmptyError)
    | proves :failsOfAMisspeltKind
        |~~ A misspelt kind answers false and is not refused. ~~|
        ~(5 | is ::Numbr | not)
    | proves :kinds/ElementKindNotTakenError/refusesAKeyword
        |~~ A keyword is no kind, and is refused at its index. ~~|
        ~(5 | is :number
          !| get [~(tag) ~(index) ~(actualTag)]
          |  eq [::any/is/kinds/ElementKindNotTakenError 0 ::keyword])
    | impl ::builtin{}

:any/get
  |~~ Answers the part of the subject a path names, read strictly: a key
      or an index that is not there is refused, `::path/KeyNotInMapError`
      with the keys near it, so a misspelled field is found where it is
      read, `{:a {:b 1}} | get /a/b` answering `1`. A key the query
      computes is read as the path of one segment, a keyword, a string or
      a number, its text the segment, `get (get /k)`, a key under a tag
      read beneath it, and any other value that is no template is
      refused, so a key the data leaves null is found where it is read;
      a boolean, null and a tag name a map files under the keyword
      `toKeyword` answers, which reads that key, `get :true`. Over a template, a literal of paths
      and quotes, it answers the template with each path read and each
      quote run against the subject where it stands, the answer written in
      the shape the template takes:
      `{:price 4 :qty 3} | get [/price /qty]` answers `[4 3]`, and
      `get {:who /first :n ~(get /tags | count)}` builds a map. A vector, a
      map or a set under a tag in a template is read and laid back under
      its tag through its kind, and any other value of it is
      answered as it is, a quote under a tag, a doc, a function and a kind among them. A literal computes nothing, so a shape computed
      is written as a template. A part that may be missing is read by
      `query`.
      ::link~(::any/query | doc) ::link~(::path | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :path
        |~~ The path read, a key of one segment, or a template of paths
            and quotes.
        ~~|
        /0 #[::keyword ::map ::number ::path ::string ::tagged ::vec]
    | throws :path/KindNotTakenError
        |~~ The path is a path, `get /a/b`, a key of one segment, a keyword,
            a number or a string, or a template, a vector, a map or a set of
            paths and quotes, or a value under a tag, a quote among them. A
            boolean, null and a tag name are no key: a map files them under
            the keyword `toKeyword` answers, which reads that key,
            `groupBy ~(gt 1) | get :true`, `get (… | toKeyword)`, and a key
            computed from data that holds null is refused where it is read.
        ~~|
    | proves :path/KindNotTakenError/refusesNull
        |~~ A key the data leaves null is refused. ~~|
        ~({:k null} | get (get /k) !| get [~(tag) ~(actualTag)] | eq [::any/get/path/KindNotTakenError ::null])
    | proves :path/KindNotTakenError/readsTheKeyOfABooleanByItsKeyword
        |~~ The group of `true` is read by its keyword. ~~|
        ~([1 2 3] | groupBy ~(gt 1) | get [~(get true !| tag) ~(get :true)] | eq [::any/get/path/KindNotTakenError [2 3]])
    | proves :readsANestedPart
        |~~ A path reads a part at any depth. ~~|
        ~({:a {:b [10 20]}} | get /a/b/-1 | eq 20)
    | proves :refusesAMissedKey
        |~~ A key that is not there is refused. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | get /b !| tag | eq ::path/KeyNotInMapError)
    | proves :readsAKeyTheQueryComputes
        |~~ A key the query computes reads as the path of one segment. ~~|
        ~({:k "b" :b 7} | get (get /k) | eq 7)
    | proves :readsTwoFieldsByATemplate
        |~~ Two fields of one record are read in one step. ~~|
        ~({:price 4 :qty 3} | get [/price /qty] | eq [4 3])
    | proves :buildsTheShapeOfItsTemplate
        |~~ A template of paths and quotes builds the answer in its shape. ~~|
        ~({:first "Ann" :tags [:a :b]} | get {:who /first :n ~(get /tags | count)} | eq {:who "Ann" :n 2})
    | proves :readsUnderATagOfTheTemplate
        |~~ A vector under a tag in a template is read and laid back under
            its tag.
        ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | get [::Box[/a]] | eq [::Box[1]])
    | proves :answersAFunctionOfTheTemplateAsItIs
        |~~ A function in a template is answered as it is. ~~|
        ~(get [::fn[~(takes :by |~~ How much. ~~| /0) ~(add by)]] | first | tag | eq ::fn)
    | proves :readsBeneathTheTags
        |~~ A path reads beneath the tags over the subject. ~~|
        ~(::Box{:w 3} | get /w | eq 3)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:any/query
  |~~ Answers the part of the subject a path names, read softly: where a
      key or an index is missing, or null stands on the way, it answers
      null, or the default the call gives, `{:a 1} | query /b 0`
      answering `0`, while a null that stands at the path is answered as
      it stands. A value on the path that has no parts is refused as
      `get` refuses it, so a path read from the wrong subject is found.
      Over a template it answers the template with each path read softly
      and each quote run, `{:a 1} | query [/a /b]` answering `[1 null]`.
      ::link~(::any/get | doc) ::link~(::any/coalesce | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :path
        |~~ The path read, a key of one segment, or a template of paths
            and quotes.
        ~~|
        /0 #[::keyword ::map ::number ::path ::string ::tagged ::vec]
    | throws :path/KindNotTakenError
        |~~ The path is a path, `query /a/b`, a key of one segment, a
            keyword, a number or a string, or a template, a vector, a map or
            a set of paths and quotes, or a value under a tag, a quote among
            them, and `query` reads any other no more softly than `get`. A
            boolean, null and a tag name are no key: a map files them under
            the keyword `toKeyword` answers, which reads that key,
            `query :true`.
        ~~|
    | proves :path/KindNotTakenError/refusesABoolean
        |~~ A boolean is no key. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | query true 7 !| tag | eq ::any/query/path/KindNotTakenError)
    | takes :default
        |~~ What a missed path answers, held and never run, null when the
            call gives none.
        ~~|
        ~(query /1 null)
    | proves :answersNullForAMissingKey
        |~~ A key that is not there answers null. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | query /b | eq null)
    | proves :answersTheDefault
        |~~ A missed path answers the default the call gives. ~~|
        ~([] | query /0 7 | eq 7)
    | proves :answersANullThatStands
        |~~ A null at the path is answered as it stands. ~~|
        ~([null] | query /0 7 | eq null)
    | proves :readsThroughNull
        |~~ A null on the way answers null. ~~|
        ~({:a null} | query /a/b | eq null)
    | proves :refusesAnotherKind
        |~~ A value on the path that has no parts is refused. ~~|
        ~({:a 5} | query /a/b !| tag | eq ::path/SubjectHasNoPartsError)
    | proves :readsATemplateSoftly
        |~~ Each path of a template is read softly. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | query [/a /b] | eq [1 null])
    | impl ::builtin{}

:any/with
  |~~ Answers its body, a template as `get` takes it, with the parts of the
      subject its names read named in each quote of the body:
      `{:id 7 :user {:name "Ann"}} | with [/id /user/name] ~(name | append (id | toString))`
      answers `"Ann7"`. The names are a vector of paths, each part named by
      the last key of its path, or a map of names and their selectors,
      each a path or a quote,
      `[::Box 5] | with {:t ~first :p ~last} [~(t) ~(p) ~(p | tag t)]`
      answering `[::Box 5 ::Box#5]`, and a map among the paths names the
      parts it maps, `with [/id {:un /user/name :gn /group/name}] …`. Each
      selector is read against the subject apart from the others, a path as
      `get` reads it and a quote run on the subject, so no selector sees the
      name of another, and each quote of the body runs on the subject as
      the quotes of a template of `get` run, the names beside the names of
      its step; no name leaves the call. A key of the subject no name names
      is read by its path, and a name that meets a verb the subject of its
      step answers is refused, as every name of a value is, `type` among
      them, which a map names otherwise, `with [/id {:t /type}] …`.
      ::link~(::any/get | doc) ::link~(::path | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :names
        |~~ The names of the parts, a vector of paths and maps, or a map of
            names and their selectors.
        ~~|
        /0 #[::map ::vec]
    | throws :names/KindNotTakenError
        |~~ The names are a vector of paths and maps,
            `with [/id /user/name] …`, or a map of names and the paths and
            quotes they read, `with {:t /0 :n ~count} …`.
        ~~|
    | throws :names/ElementKindNotTakenError
        |~~ Each element of the names is a path, which names its part by its
            last key, or a map of names and their selectors; a quote alone
            names no part, and a map gives it a name, `[{:t ~first}]`.
        ~~|
        ::fn/ElementKindNotTakenError
    | throws :names/SelectorKindNotTakenError
        |~~ A name reads its part by a path or a quote, `{:t /0 :n ~count}`;
            a value of the data reads no part, so a map of the data names no
            parts, and its keys are named by their paths, `[/a /b]`.
        ~~|
        ~(wires :name |~~ The name asked for, a keyword or a tag. ~~| #[::keyword ::tag]
          | wires :expected |~~ The kinds taken, where a value of none of them was refused. ~~| ::set
          | wires :actualTag |~~ The kind of the value refused. ~~| ::tag
          | wires :actualValue |~~ The value refused. ~~|)
    | throws :names/KeyNotNameError
        |~~ A name is a name of one segment, as a step calls it: a last key
            that is an index, a quoted key or a word of the literals,
            `/items/0`, `/"first name"`, `/null`, names no part, and a map
            gives the part a name, `[{:first /items/0}]`.
        ~~|
        ~(wires :name |~~ The name asked for, a keyword or a tag. ~~| #[::keyword ::tag]
          | wires :actualTag |~~ The kind of the value refused. ~~| ::tag
          | wires :actualValue |~~ The value refused. ~~|)
    | throws :names/NameWrittenTwiceError
        |~~ A name names one part: two paths that end in one key,
            `[/a/name /b/name]`, name their parts by a map,
            `[{:an /a/name :bn /b/name}]`.
        ~~|
        ~(wires :name |~~ The name asked for, a keyword or a tag. ~~| #[::keyword ::tag]
          | wires :actualTag |~~ The kind of the value refused. ~~| ::tag
          | wires :actualValue |~~ The value refused. ~~|)
    | takes :body
        |~~ The template answered, as `get` takes it: a quote, a path, or a
            vector, a map or a set of them.
        ~~|
        /1 #[::keyword ::map ::number ::path ::string ::tagged ::vec]
    | throws :body/KindNotTakenError
        |~~ The body is a template as `get` takes it, a quote, a path or a
            shape of them, `with [/a] ~(a | add 1)`.
        ~~|
    | proves :namesAPartByTheLastKeyOfItsPath
        |~~ A path names its part by its last key. ~~|
        ~({:id 7 :user {:name "Ann"}} | with [/id /user/name] ~(name | append (id | toString)) | eq "Ann7")
    | proves :namesThePartsAMapReadsForEachBranch
        |~~ A map names the parts its paths and quotes read, in every
            branch of the body.
        ~~|
        ~([::Box 5] | with {:t ~first :p ~last} [~(t) ~(p) ~(p | tag t)] | eq [::Box 5 ::Box#5])
    | proves :namesByAMapAmongThePaths
        |~~ A map among the paths names the parts it maps. ~~|
        ~({:id 7 :user {:name "Ann"}} | with [/id {:who /user/name}] ~(who | append (id | toString)) | eq "Ann7")
    | proves :readsAPathStrictlyAndAQuoteAsItIsWritten
        |~~ A path reads its part as `get` reads it, and a quote as it is
            written.
        ~~|
        ~({:id 7} | get [~(with {:t /type} ~(t) !| tag) ~(with {:t ~(query /type "none")} ~(t))] | eq [::path/KeyNotInMapError "none"])
    | proves :readsEachSelectorApart
        |~~ A selector sees no name of another selector. ~~|
        ~([::Box 5] | with [{:t ~first} {:p ~(t)}] ~(p) !| name | eq :t)
    | proves :runsTheBodyOnTheSubject
        |~~ The body runs on the subject, its other parts read by their
            paths.
        ~~|
        ~({:id 7 :n 2} | with [/id] ~(get /n | add id) | eq 9)
    | proves :leavesNoNameAfterTheCall
        |~~ No name leaves the call. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | with [/a] ~(a) | a !| name | eq :a)
    | proves :meetsAVerbOfTheSubjectAloud
        |~~ A name that meets a verb of the subject is refused, and a map
            names its part otherwise.
        ~~|
        ~({:type "user" :id 7} | get [~(with [/id /type] ~(id | toString | append type) !| tag) ~(with [/id {:t /type}] ~(id | toString | append t))] | eq [::call/ValueMeetsVerbError "7user"])
    | proves :names/KindNotTakenError/refusesAPath
        |~~ A path alone is no names. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | with /a ~(a) !| tag | eq ::any/with/names/KindNotTakenError)
    | proves :names/ElementKindNotTakenError/refusesAQuoteAlone
        |~~ A quote alone among the names is refused at its index. ~~|
        ~([::Box 5] | with [{:t /0} ~first] ~(t) !| get [~(tag) ~(index)] | eq [::any/with/names/ElementKindNotTakenError 1])
    | proves :names/SelectorKindNotTakenError/refusesAMapOfTheData
        |~~ A map of the data names no parts. ~~|
        ~({:a 1 :b 2} | with it ~(a | mul b) !| tag | eq ::any/with/names/SelectorKindNotTakenError)
    | proves :names/KeyNotNameError/refusesAnIndex
        |~~ A path that ends in an index names no part. ~~|
        ~({:items [5 6]} | with [/items/0] ~(it) !| get [~(tag) ~(name)] | eq [::any/with/names/KeyNotNameError :"0"])
    | proves :names/NameWrittenTwiceError/refusesTwoPathsOfOneKey
        |~~ Two paths that end in one key name one part twice. ~~|
        ~({:a {:name 1} :b {:name 2}} | with [/a/name /b/name] ~(name) !| get [~(tag) ~(name)] | eq [::any/with/names/NameWrittenTwiceError :name])
    | proves :body/KindNotTakenError/refusesABoolean
        |~~ A boolean is no template. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | with [/a] true !| tag | eq ::any/with/body/KindNotTakenError)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:any/put
  |~~ Answers the subject with a value laid at the path the call gives:
      a key the last map on the path lacks is added, and a key it holds or
      an index a vector holds is replaced, `{:a {:b 1}} | put /a/c 2`
      answering `{:a {:b 1 :c 2}}`. The path is followed as `get` follows
      it, so a key or an index missing on the way is refused where the
      path writes it, and a value under a tag on the way is laid under it
      again through its kind. A key the query
      computes is the path of one segment, `put :a 1`, and a vector grows
      by `append`.
      ::link~(::any/update | doc) ::link~(::any/delete | doc)
      ::link~(::any/get | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :path
        |~~ The path written, or a key of one segment. ~~|
        /0 #[::keyword ::number ::path ::string]
    | throws :path/KindNotTakenError
        |~~ The path is a path, `put /a/b 2`, or a key of one segment, a
            keyword, a string or a number, `put :a 2`.
        ~~|
    | proves :path/KindNotTakenError/refusesABoolean
        |~~ A path that is no path and no key is refused. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | put true 2 !| tag | eq ::any/put/path/KindNotTakenError)
    | takes :value |~~ The value laid at the path. ~~| /1
    | proves :addsAKey
        |~~ A key the last map lacks is added. ~~|
        ~({:a {:b 1}} | put /a/c 2 | eq {:a {:b 1 :c 2}})
    | proves :replacesTheElementAtAnIndex
        |~~ The element at an index a vector holds is replaced. ~~|
        ~([1 2 3] | put /-1 9 | eq [1 2 9])
    | proves :refusesAKeyMissedOnTheWay
        |~~ A key missing on the way is refused where the path writes it.
        ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | put /b/c 2 !| get [~(tag) ~(path)] | eq [::path/KeyNotInMapError /b/c])
    | proves :refusesAnIndexPastTheEnd
        |~~ A vector grows by `append`, not by an index past its end. ~~|
        ~([1 2] | put /2 3 !| tag | eq ::path/IndexOutOfBoundsError)
    | proves :laysTheTagsAgain
        |~~ A value under a tag is laid under it again. ~~|
        ~(::Box{:w 2} | put /w 3 | eq ::Box{:w 3})
    | proves :laysEveryTagAgain
        |~~ A value under two tags is laid under both again. ~~|
        ~(::A::B{:w 2} | put /w 3 | eq ::A::B{:w 3})
    | proves :refusesAValueWithoutParts
        |~~ A value on the path that has no parts is refused, as `get`
            refuses it.
        ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | put /a/b 2 !| tag | eq ::path/SubjectHasNoPartsError)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:any/update
  |~~ Answers the subject with the part at the path the call gives laid
      anew by what the code the call gives answers for it,
      `{:n 1} | update /n ~(add 1)` answering `{:n 2}`. The part must be
      there, as for `get`, an error the code answers is the answer, and
      the tags on the way are laid again as `put` lays them.
      ::link~(::any/put | doc) ::link~(::tagged/within | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :path
        |~~ The path written, or a key of one segment. ~~|
        /0 #[::keyword ::number ::path ::string]
    | throws :path/KindNotTakenError
        |~~ The path is a path, `update /n ~(add 1)`, or a key of one
            segment, `update :n ~(add 1)`.
        ~~|
    | proves :path/KindNotTakenError/refusesABoolean
        |~~ A path that is no path and no key is refused. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | update true ~(it) !| tag | eq ::any/update/path/KindNotTakenError)
    | takes :code
        |~~ The code run on the part, a quote or a path. ~~|
        /1 #[::path ::quote]
    | throws :code/KindNotTakenError
        |~~ The code is a quote, `update /n ~(add 1)`, or a path,
            `update /n /value`; written without the tilde,
            `update /n (add 1)`, it is computed once at the call and
            refused.
        ~~|
    | proves :code/KindNotTakenError/refusesANumber
        |~~ Code that is no quote and no path is refused. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | update /a 5 !| tag | eq ::any/update/code/KindNotTakenError)
    | proves :runsTheCodeOnThePart
        |~~ The part at the path is laid anew by what the code answers. ~~|
        ~({:a {:n 1}} | update /a/n ~(add 1) | eq {:a {:n 2}})
    | proves :refusesAMissedPart
        |~~ A part that is not there is refused. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | update /b ~(add 1) !| tag | eq ::path/KeyNotInMapError)
    | proves :answersTheErrorOfItsCode
        |~~ An error the code answers is the answer. ~~|
        ~({:n "x"} | update /n ~(add 1) !| tag | eq ::number/add/SubjectNotNumberError)
    | proves :answersTheErrorBeneathTheTags
        |~~ An error the code answers beneath a tag is the answer, laid
            under none.
        ~~|
        ~(::A::B{:n "x"} | update /n ~(add 1) !| tag | eq ::number/add/SubjectNotNumberError)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:any/delete
  |~~ Answers the subject without the part at the path the call gives: a
      key taken out of a map, or an element out of a vector by its index,
      the elements after it moving up, `{:a 1 :b 2} | delete /b` answering `{:a 1}`.
      The part must be there, as for `get`, so a misspelled key is found;
      keys that may be absent leave a map by `minus`. The tags on the way
      are laid again as `put` lays them.
      ::link~(::any/put | doc) ::link~(::map/minus | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :path
        |~~ The path of the part taken out, or a key of one segment. ~~|
        /0 #[::keyword ::number ::path ::string]
    | throws :path/KindNotTakenError
        |~~ The path is a path, `delete /a/b`, or a key of one segment,
            `delete :a`.
        ~~|
    | proves :path/KindNotTakenError/refusesABoolean
        |~~ A path that is no path and no key is refused. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | delete true !| tag | eq ::any/delete/path/KindNotTakenError)
    | proves :takesAKeyOut
        |~~ A key is taken out of the map at the path. ~~|
        ~({:a {:b 1 :c 2}} | delete /a/b | eq {:a {:c 2}})
    | proves :takesAnElementOut
        |~~ An element is taken out of a vector by its index, the elements
            after it moving up.
        ~~|
        ~([1 2 3] | delete /0 | eq [2 3])
    | proves :refusesAMissedKey
        |~~ A key that is not there is refused. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | delete /b !| tag | eq ::path/KeyNotInMapError)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:any/it
  |~~ Answers the value in the pipe, the subject itself: `:r it` names
      the record a later step reads back, `get [~(it) ~(count)]` holds it beside
      what it counts, and `5 | mul it` squares it.
      ::link~(::qlang/names | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | proves :answersTheSubject
        |~~ The subject itself multiplies the subject. ~~|
        ~(5 | mul it | eq 25)
    | proves :namesTheRecord
        |~~ A name keeps the record for a later step. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | :r it | get /a | add (r | get /a) | eq 2)
    | impl ~()

:any/ok
  |~~ Answers `true`. A step after a failure is skipped, so `ok !| false`
      answers whether the step before it stood: `5 | ok !| false`
      answers `true`, and a failure before it `false`.
      ::link~(::qlang/errors | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | proves :asksWhetherTheStepBeforeStood
        |~~ `ok !| false` tells a value from a failure. ~~|
        ~(get [~(5 | ok !| false) ~(!{:k 1} | raise | ok !| false)] | eq [true false])
    | impl ~(true)

:any/raise
  |~~ Answers the value as an error on the fail track, an error at rest,
      `!{:reason :late} | raise`, among them, so a query fails where its
      own check fails. The value is a map beneath its tags, every entry a
      fact, and its tag is the outermost of the tags over the map,
      `::Oops{:message "boom"} | raise`, the tag of a refusal over its
      error at rest among them, or `::error`; an error at rest, raised
      again, is the error it was.
      A map that writes a `:trace` lays it under `::trace`, which refuses
      a value that is no vector of frames, and the raise resumes it, while
      an empty `:trace` is no trace and a map that writes none holds none,
      so its raise starts one at its own step. `|` steps around the
      failure, writing its trace, and `!|` catches it, its step running on
      the error at rest. It takes no value: what the error says is a fact
      of the map. A value of another kind is put in a map first,
      `get {:value ~(it)} | raise`.
      ::link~(::error | doc) ::link~(::fail | doc) ::link~(::trace | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns
        |~~ The error the value is raised as, on the fail track. ~~|
        ::error
    | throws :SubjectKindNotTakenError
        |~~ `raise` raises a map, beneath the tags over it: a value of
            another kind is put in one first, `get {:value ~(it)} | raise`,
            or the error is written as its literal, `!{:reason :late} | raise`.
        ~~|
        ~(wires :expected |~~ The kind raised, `#[::map]`. ~~| ::set
          | wires :actualTag |~~ The kind of the subject refused: the first frame of the trace holds the subject. ~~| ::tag)
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `raise` takes no value: what the error says is a fact of the
            map, `{:message "boom"} | raise`.
        ~~|
    | proves :raisesTheSubject
        |~~ `{…} | raise` raises the subject, a map the step builds among
            them.
        ~~|
        ~({:message "boom"} | raise !| get /message | eq "boom")
    | proves :takesTheTagTheMapCarries
        |~~ The tag the map carries is the tag of the error. ~~|
        ~(::Oops{:message "boom"} | raise !| tag | eq ::Oops)
    | proves :keepsEveryEntryAsAFact
        |~~ Every entry of the map is a fact of the error. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | raise !| get [~(tag) /a] | eq [::error 1])
    | proves :raisesAnErrorAtRestAsItself
        |~~ An error at rest raised again is the error it was. ~~|
        ~("x" | add 1 !| raise !| tag | eq ::number/add/SubjectNotNumberError)
    | proves :startsATraceOverAnEmptyTrace
        |~~ An empty `:trace` is no trace: the raise starts one, and the
            steps the failure skips join its frame.
        ~~|
        ~({:a 1 :trace []} | raise | count !| get /trace | map ~(get [~(failed) ~(skipped)]) | eq [[~(raise) ~(count)]])
    | proves :startsATraceAtTheRaise
        |~~ An error raised without a trace starts its trace at the raise.
        ~~|
        ~(!{:a 1} | raise | count !| minus #[:trace] | raise | add 1
          !| get /trace | map ~failed | eq [~(raise)])
    | proves :resumesTheTraceItWrites
        |~~ A map that writes a vector of frames resumes it, laid under
            `::trace`.
        ~~|
        ~({:trace [::frame[1 ~(pre)]]} | raise !| get /trace | eq ::trace[::frame[1 ~(pre)]])
    | proves :laysTheTraceItWritesThroughItsKind
        |~~ A trace that is no vector of frames is refused by its kind, a
            vector of maps at the index of its first.
        ~~|
        ~({:trace [{:failed ~(a) :subject 1}]} | raise !| get [~(tag) ~(index)] | eq [::trace/ElementKindNotTakenError 0])
    | proves :renamesByATag
        |~~ A step that lays a tag over an error caught renames it. ~~|
        ~("x" | add 1 !| tag ::Renamed | raise !| tag | eq ::Renamed)
    | proves :guardsAValue
        |~~ A guard raises an error of its own tag when a value fails its
            check.
        ~~|
        ~(42 | if (gt 100) ~(it) ~(get {:value ~(it)} | tag ::TooSmall | raise)
          !| get [~(tag) /value]
          |  eq [::TooSmall 42])
    | proves :SubjectKindNotTakenError/refusesANumber
        |~~ A number is no map to raise. ~~|
        ~(42 | raise !| get [~(tag) ~(expected) ~(actualTag)] | eq [::any/raise/SubjectKindNotTakenError #[::map] ::number])
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesAMessage
        |~~ A message given to `raise` is refused; the map holds it. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | raise "boom" !| tag | eq ::any/raise/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | impl ::builtin{}


:any/paths
  |~~ Answers the leaves of a value, a set of pairs, each the path `get`
      reads a leaf by and its kind:
      `{:a {:b 1 :c [2 "x"]}} | paths` answers
      `#[[/a/b ::number] [/a/c/0 ::number] [/a/c/1 ::string]]`. A part
      under a tag is a leaf of its kind, which tells of itself on its
      page, a quote and a doc among them; a set opens as a vector does,
      its elements read by their indices; an empty vector or map is a part
      that holds none, and the subject is read beneath the tags over it.
      `shape` folds the leaves of every element into one shape.
      ::link~(::any/shape | doc) ::link~(::any/get | doc)
      ::link~(::path | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ The pairs of the path and the kind of each leaf. ~~| ::set
    | proves :answersTheLeaves
        |~~ The pairs hold the path and the kind of every leaf. ~~|
        ~({:a {:b 1 :c [2 "x"]} :d []} | paths | eq #[[/a/b ::number] [/a/c/0 ::number] [/a/c/1 ::string] [/d ::vec]])
    | proves :answersNoPathOfAValueWithoutParts
        |~~ A value that has no parts has no path in it. ~~|
        ~(5 | paths | eq #[])
    | proves :readsBeneathTheTags
        |~~ The leaves are read beneath the tags stacked over the subject.
        ~~|
        ~(::A::B{:w 2} | paths | eq #[[/w ::number]])
    | proves :standsAtATag
        |~~ A part under a tag is a leaf of its kind. ~~|
        ~({:q ~(add 1) :d |~~ d ~~| :t ::Box{:w 1}} | paths | eq #[[/d ::doc] [/q ::quote] [/t ::Box]])
    | proves :readsASetAsItsVector
        |~~ A set is read as its vector, its elements by their indices. ~~|
        ~({:s #[1 2]} | paths | eq #[[/s/0 ::number] [/s/1 ::number]])
    | proves :eachReadsByGet
        |~~ Each path `paths` answers reads its leaf. ~~|
        ~({:a {:b 1}} | :v it | paths | map ~(:p (get /0) | (v | get p)) | eq [1])
    | impl ::builtin{}

:any/shape
  |~~ Answers the shape of a value, its structure beneath its tags as a
      value of `::shape`, whose page tells how a shape reads:
      `{:users [{:name "a" :age 1} {:name "b" :age "2"}]} | shape` answers
      `::shape{:users [{:name ::string :age #[::number ::string]}]}`.
      ::link~(::shape | doc) ::link~(::any/paths | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ The shape of the value. ~~| ::shape
    | proves :answersTheKindOfAnAtom
        |~~ An atom answers its kind. ~~|
        ~([5 "a" null :k /a] | map ~shape | eq [::shape::number ::shape::string ::shape::null ::shape::keyword ::shape::path])
    | proves :keepsTheKeysOfAMap
        |~~ A map keeps its keys, each with its shape. ~~|
        ~({:a 1 :b {:c "x"}} | shape | eq ::shape{:a ::number :b {:c ::string}})
    | proves :foldsTheElementsOfAVector
        |~~ A vector holds one element whose shape holds of all, an empty
            one marking the element of the rest absent.
        ~~|
        ~([[1 2] [] [3]] | shape | eq ::shape[[::absent::number]])
    | proves :readsTuplesIndexByIndex
        |~~ Vectors of one length that part index by index are tuples. ~~|
        ~([["a" 1] ["b" null]] | shape | eq ::shape[[::string ::nullable::number]])
    | proves :mergesRecordsWhateverTheirOrder
        |~~ Records that share keys merge whatever their order. ~~|
        ~([{:a 1} {:b 2} {:a "x" :b 3}] | shape | eq ([{:a "x" :b 3} {:a 1} {:b 2}] | shape))
    | proves :setsTheKindsWhereElementsPart
        |~~ Where the elements part, the shape is the set of their kinds.
        ~~|
        ~([1 "a" 2] | shape | eq ::shape[#[::number ::string]])
    | proves :mergesRecordsThatShareAKey
        |~~ Records that share a key merge, a key some lack absent and a
            part null in some nullable.
        ~~|
        ~([{:a 1} {:a null :b "x"}] | shape | eq ::shape[{:a ::nullable::number :b ::absent::string}])
    | proves :keepsRecordsOfNoCommonKeyApart
        |~~ Records that share no key stand apart. ~~|
        ~([{:a 1} {:b "x"}] | shape | eq ::shape[#[{:a ::number} {:b ::string}]])
    | proves :standsAtATag
        |~~ A part under a tag stands as its tag. ~~|
        ~({:t ::Box{:w 1} :q ~(add 1)} | shape | eq ::shape{:t ::Box :q ::quote})
    | proves :shapesASetByOneElement
        |~~ A set holds one element whose shape holds of all of them. ~~|
        ~(#[1 2] | shape | eq ::shape#[::number])
    | proves :readsBeneathTheTagsOfItsSubject
        |~~ The subject is read beneath its tags. ~~|
        ~(::Box{:w 1} | shape | eq ::shape{:w ::number})
    | proves :showsNoElementOfAnEmptyVector
        |~~ An empty vector shows no element. ~~|
        ~([] | shape | eq ::shape[])
    | proves :readsAListByItsOneElement
        |~~ A list of a shape reads by its one element. ~~|
        ~({:users [{:age 1} {:age "2"}]} | shape | get /users/0/age | eq #[::number ::string])
    | impl ::builtin{}

